"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdocs=self.webpackChunkdocs||[]).push([[8130],{77735:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"how-to-add-a-favicon-in-html","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/how-to-add-a-favicon-in-html","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-20-add-favicon-html/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-20-add-favicon-html/index.md","title":"How to Add a Favicon in HTML","description":"Add a favicon to your HTML pages, choose the right icon path for a site in a subfolder, and check it from a browser tab.","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"HTML","permalink":"/blog/tags/html"}],"readingTime":3.17,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null,"socials":{}}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"how-to-add-a-favicon-in-html","title":"How to Add a Favicon in HTML","description":"Add a favicon to your HTML pages, choose the right icon path for a site in a subfolder, and check it from a browser tab.","image":"/tutorials/media/favicon-generator-tab-previews.png","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","authors":[{"key":"charly","name":"Charly Abraham"}],"tags":["Tools","HTML"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"How to Change an SVG Icon\u2019s Fill and Stroke Colors","permalink":"/blog/change-svg-fill-and-stroke"}},"content":"A favicon is the small picture beside a page title in a browser tab. To add one, put the icon file on your website and add a `<link rel=\\"icon\\">` inside each page\u2019s `<head>`. The `href` tells the browser where to find the file.\\n\\nYou\u2019ll need an HTML page you can edit and a logo, or you can use the sample image below. We\u2019ll give a site called Trail Notes the same icon on its home page and its About page, one folder deeper.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Make the icon files\\n\\n1. Open the free [Phoenix Code Favicon Generator](https://phcode.dev/tools/favicon-generator.html). Click **Choose image** to open a PNG, JPG, or WebP file. To follow along without artwork, click **Try a sample**.\\n2. Look at **Light tab** and **Dark tab**. Both show the icon at 16 \xd7 16 pixels. If the lettering disappears at this size, try a single letter or a simpler shape.\\n3. Click **Download icons** and extract `favicon.zip`. Copy `favicon.ico`, `favicon-96x96.png`, and `apple-touch-icon.png` into the folder that holds your home page. The ZIP also includes a `README.txt` you can keep for reference.\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/favicon-generator-tab-previews.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/favicon-generator-tab-previews.png\\" width=\\"574\\" height=\\"512\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"Favicon Generator shows the sample letter F beside the page title on light and dark tabs, above the Download icons button.\\" /></a>\\n\\n## Link the files from each HTML page\\n\\nIn the generator, scroll to **Add it to your website** and click **Copy HTML**. The links start with `/`, which means the top of your domain. Check the address where your icon will be published before pasting them.\\n\\n| Where the icon file opens | What to use |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| `https://example.com/favicon.ico` | Use the generator\u2019s HTML as copied. |\\n| `https://example.com/trail-notes/favicon.ico` | Add `/trail-notes/` before each filename as shown below. Substitute your own public folder name. |\\n\\nFor the second case, the project folder contains these files.\\n\\n```text\\ntrail-notes/\\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 index.html\\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 favicon.ico\\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 favicon-96x96.png\\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 apple-touch-icon.png\\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 guides/\\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 about.html\\n```\\n\\nOpen `index.html` in your editor. Place these three lines just before `</head>`. If the page already has icon links, update their paths. Do the same in `guides/about.html`, then save both files and publish them along with the icons.\\n\\n```html\\n<link rel=\\"icon\\" href=\\"/trail-notes/favicon.ico\\" sizes=\\"16x16 32x32 48x48\\">\\n<link rel=\\"icon\\" type=\\"image/png\\" href=\\"/trail-notes/favicon-96x96.png\\" sizes=\\"96x96\\">\\n<link rel=\\"apple-touch-icon\\" href=\\"/trail-notes/apple-touch-icon.png\\" sizes=\\"180x180\\">\\n```\\n\\nThe first two links give browsers a choice of tab icons. The last supplies the icon for an Apple home-screen shortcut. Both HTML pages can use the same links because each address starts at the domain root.\\n\\n## Check the icon on both pages\\n\\n1. Open the published home page in its own browser tab. Look to the left of its title for your icon.\\n2. Open the nested About page in a second tab. It should show the same icon. If only the home page works, look for `href=\\"favicon.ico\\"` on the About page. That spelling makes the browser look for `guides/favicon.ico`. Use the shared path from the example.\\n3. If the icon is missing on both pages, open its address directly on your own domain. For Trail Notes, that path is `/trail-notes/favicon-96x96.png`. You should see the image. A missing-page message or your site\u2019s home page means the image is unavailable at that address.\\n\\nIf the image opens directly but the tab still shows an old icon, try a fresh private window. To see which icon URL the browser requested, open developer tools, select **Network**, and reload the page. [Check a failed resource in an HTML preview](/blog/html-preview-missing-css-images-javascript#check-a-failed-resource)."},{"id":"change-svg-fill-and-stroke","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/change-svg-fill-and-stroke","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-20-change-svg-colors/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-20-change-svg-colors/index.md","title":"How to Change an SVG Icon\u2019s Fill and Stroke Colors","description":"Recolor the inside and outline of an inline SVG separately. Try a trail-marker icon in the Phoenix HTML Editor, then download and reopen the finished page.","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"HTML","permalink":"/blog/tags/html"},{"inline":true,"label":"SVG","permalink":"/blog/tags/svg"}],"readingTime":2.23,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null,"socials":{}}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"change-svg-fill-and-stroke","title":"How to Change an SVG Icon\u2019s Fill and Stroke Colors","description":"Recolor the inside and outline of an inline SVG separately. Try a trail-marker icon in the Phoenix HTML Editor, then download and reopen the finished page.","image":"/tutorials/media/svg-fill-stroke-result.png","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","authors":[{"key":"charly","name":"Charly Abraham"}],"tags":["Tools","HTML","SVG"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Add a Favicon in HTML","permalink":"/blog/how-to-add-a-favicon-in-html"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Compare Two JSON Files and Read the Differences","permalink":"/blog/compare-two-json-files"}},"content":"To change the color inside an SVG shape, edit its `fill`. To change the line around it, edit its `stroke`. Try both on the trail marker below. You\u2019ll start with an orange circle and a white border, then save a green circle with a dark border. The white arrow in the middle keeps its color.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Open the practice icon\\n\\nOpen the [Phoenix HTML Editor](https://phcode.dev/tools/html-editor.html). In the **index.html** tab, replace the example code with the block below. Leave **Auto-run** on, or click **Run** after an edit. If your screen shows separate panels, select **Preview** to see the icon.\\n\\n```html\\n<!doctype html>\\n<html lang=\\"en\\">\\n<head><title>Trail marker</title></head>\\n<body>\\n  <svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"\\n       width=\\"160\\" height=\\"160\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 80 80\\"\\n       role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Trail marker pointing right\\">\\n    <circle cx=\\"40\\" cy=\\"40\\" r=\\"32\\"\\n            fill=\\"#ff8c42\\" stroke=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke-width=\\"6\\" />\\n    <path d=\\"M29 40h22m-8-8 8 8-8 8\\"\\n          fill=\\"none\\" stroke=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke-width=\\"4\\"\\n          stroke-linecap=\\"round\\" stroke-linejoin=\\"round\\" />\\n  </svg>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n```\\n\\n## Change one part at a time\\n\\n1. Find the line with **`<circle`**. Change its `fill` from `#ff8c42` to `#237a58`. The disc turns green. The arrow stays white.\\n2. On that same circle, change `stroke=\\"#ffffff\\"` to `stroke=\\"#17382e\\"`. A dark green ring appears around the disc. Keep `stroke-width=\\"6\\"` so the outline stays visible.\\n3. Look at the **`<path`** beneath the circle. This is the arrow. Its `fill=\\"none\\"` keeps its interior empty, and its white `stroke` draws the line. To recolor only the arrow in your own version, edit the stroke on this path.\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/svg-fill-stroke-result.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/svg-fill-stroke-result.png\\" width=\\"711\\" height=\\"310\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"The HTML Editor preview shows the completed trail icon with a green disc, dark outline, and white arrow pointing right.\\" /></a>\\n\\nClick **Download** (the downward arrow) in the editor toolbar. Open the downloaded **index.html** in your browser. The green disc, dark ring, and white arrow should appear in that copy too. [Open a downloaded HTML page in Chrome](/blog/open-an-html-file-in-chrome)\\n\\n## My HTML only shows an image tag\\n\\nIf the page contains `<img src=\\"images/marker.svg\\" alt=\\"Trail marker\\">`, the shapes live in **images/marker.svg**. Open that file in your code editor, find the shape, edit its fill or stroke there, and save. Reload the page that uses the image. A CSS `fill` on the outer `<img>` won\u2019t reach the circle inside the separate file. [SVG fill and stroke](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/SVG/Tutorials/SVG_from_scratch/Fills_and_strokes)"},{"id":"compare-two-json-files","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/compare-two-json-files","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-20-compare-json-files/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-20-compare-json-files/index.md","title":"How to Compare Two JSON Files and Read the Differences","description":"Compare two JSON files in your browser. Follow a worked example to find added, removed, and changed values and see how array order affects the result.","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"JSON","permalink":"/blog/tags/json"}],"readingTime":2.58,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null,"socials":{}}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"compare-two-json-files","title":"How to Compare Two JSON Files and Read the Differences","description":"Compare two JSON files in your browser. Follow a worked example to find added, removed, and changed values and see how array order affects the result.","image":"/tutorials/media/json-compare-four-changes.png","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","authors":[{"key":"charly","name":"Charly Abraham"}],"tags":["Tools","JSON"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Change an SVG Icon\u2019s Fill and Stroke Colors","permalink":"/blog/change-svg-fill-and-stroke"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Decode Base64 Text and Preview an Encoded Image","permalink":"/blog/decode-base64-text-and-images"}},"content":"Did a setting change, or did someone just rearrange the file? Open the old version in **Original JSON** and the new version in **Changed JSON**, then click **Compare JSON**. Phoenix Code compares the values and shows the location of each difference. Spaces, indentation, and the order of keys in an object can change without showing up as edits.\\n\\nYou can use your own two files or paste the examples below. The free [JSON Compare & Diff tool](https://phcode.dev/tools/json-compare.html) runs in your browser.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Compare the old and new settings\\n\\nOpen [JSON Compare & Diff](https://phcode.dev/tools/json-compare.html). If you have your own files, click **Open file** in **Original JSON** and choose the older file. Then use **Open file** in **Changed JSON** to choose the newer one.\\n\\nTo try it with Trail Notes, paste the first block into **Original JSON** and the second into **Changed JSON**.\\n\\n**Original JSON**\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"project\\": \\"Trail Notes\\",\\n  \\"published\\": false,\\n  \\"pages\\": [\\"home\\", \\"walks\\"],\\n  \\"theme\\": {\\"color\\": \\"green\\", \\"logo\\": \\"trail.svg\\"}\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Changed JSON**\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"theme\\": {\\"color\\": \\"green\\", \\"accent\\": \\"amber\\"},\\n  \\"pages\\": [\\"home\\", \\"walks\\", \\"map\\"],\\n  \\"published\\": true,\\n  \\"project\\": \\"Trail Notes\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Read what changed\\n\\nClick **Compare JSON**. For Trail Notes, the message reads **Compared: 4 differences found.** The toolbar shows **2 added \xb7 1 removed \xb7 1 changed**.\\n\\nUse the **up and down arrows** beside the difference count to move through the results. Each stop gives you a path. For example, `/pages/2` means the third item in `pages`, because array positions start at zero.\\n\\n| Path | What happened in Trail Notes |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| `/published` | The existing setting changed from `false` to `true`. |\\n| `/pages/2` | `\\"map\\"` was added after `\\"walks\\"`. |\\n| `/theme/logo` | The `logo` field and its value `\\"trail.svg\\"` were removed. |\\n| `/theme/accent` | A new `accent` field was added with the value `\\"amber\\"`. |\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/json-compare-four-changes.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/json-compare-four-changes.png\\" width=\\"1250\\" height=\\"625\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"The second of four differences is selected. The Added label identifies /pages/2, and the Changed JSON pane highlights the new map item.\\" /></a>\\n\\nClick **Copy differences** to keep a text report. It uses `+` for an addition, `-` for a removal, and `~` for a changed value. If an addition shows up as a removal, check which version you opened in **Original JSON**.\\n\\n## Why moving a key is different from moving an array item\\n\\nThe `project` key appears first in the original and last in the changed file. Its value is still `\\"Trail Notes\\"`, so it has no entry in the report. An object identifies each value by its key.\\n\\nAn array identifies items by position. To see this, replace the original pane with `{\\"pages\\":[\\"home\\",\\"walks\\"]}` and the changed pane with `{\\"pages\\":[\\"walks\\",\\"home\\"]}`. Click **Compare JSON** again. You\u2019ll get **2 changed**. Position `/pages/0` now holds `\\"walks\\"`, and `/pages/1` now holds `\\"home\\"`.\\n\\nTo apply a change, open the source file in your editor, edit that setting, and save. Then open the saved version in **Changed JSON** and compare again. If you\u2019re editing a project configuration in Phoenix Code, see [JSON editing and error checks](/docs/Features/json-code-intelligence)."},{"id":"decode-base64-text-and-images","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/decode-base64-text-and-images","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-20-decode-base64/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-20-decode-base64/index.md","title":"How to Decode Base64 Text and Preview an Encoded Image","description":"Decode a UTF-8 message, check an invalid Base64 string, and display a PNG data URL in an HTML preview. Both examples work in your browser.","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"HTML","permalink":"/blog/tags/html"}],"readingTime":2.01,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null,"socials":{}}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"decode-base64-text-and-images","title":"How to Decode Base64 Text and Preview an Encoded Image","description":"Decode a UTF-8 message, check an invalid Base64 string, and display a PNG data URL in an HTML preview. Both examples work in your browser.","image":"/tutorials/media/base64-text-decoded.png","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","authors":[{"key":"charly","name":"Charly Abraham"}],"tags":["Tools","HTML"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Compare Two JSON Files and Read the Differences","permalink":"/blog/compare-two-json-files"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Change All Caps to Sentence Case on a Website","permalink":"/blog/change-all-caps-website-text"}},"content":"If you have a Base64-encoded message, paste it into **Base64 Encoded** in the Phoenix decoder and click **Decode**. If you copied something starting with `data:image/png;base64,` from an HTML file, you have a picture. Here\u2019s how to read the message and see the picture.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Decode a text message\\n\\n1. Open the [Phoenix Base64 Encoder & Decoder](https://phcode.dev/tools/base64-encode-decode.html). Find the box labeled **Base64 Encoded**.\\n2. Copy this whole string into the box, including the two equals signs at the end.\\n\\n   ```text\\n   Q2Fmw6kgYnkgdGhlIHRyYWlsLg==\\n   ```\\n\\n3. Click **Decode** below that box. The **Text** box should now read **Caf\xe9 by the trail.** The accent in *Caf\xe9* is part of the original UTF-8 text. Click **Copy** beside **Text** to keep the readable version.\\n4. To check it the other way, click **Encode** beneath the text. You should get the original string back, including its ending.\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/base64-text-decoded.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/base64-text-decoded.png\\" width=\\"1052\\" height=\\"390\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"The Text box contains Caf\xe9 by the trail. and the Base64 Encoded box contains the matching encoded string.\\" /></a>\\n\\nIf the tool reports **Invalid Base64 string**, recopy the complete value. For example, `Q2Fm%` produces that error. Check for punctuation copied from the surrounding sentence or quotation marks from the HTML. A Base64 string by itself doesn\u2019t tell you which kind of file it contains.\\n\\n## See an image copied from HTML\\n\\nIn a data URL, the part before the comma describes what follows. In `data:image/png;base64,...`, the type is PNG and the rest represents the image bytes. Keep the prefix and the bytes together when you use the URL as an image source. [Data URL syntax](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/URI/Reference/Schemes/data)\\n\\nOpen the [Phoenix HTML Viewer](https://phcode.dev/tools/html-viewer.html) and paste this complete image tag into **HTML source**. If the Viewer shows tabs, click **Preview**.\\n\\n```html\\n<img width=\\"64\\" height=\\"64\\" alt=\\"Green square\\"\\n  src=\\"data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAIAAACQd1PeAAAACXBIWXMAAAPoAAAD6AG1e1JrAAAADElEQVQImWPQqwkCAAHYAP0XYDMkAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC\\">\\n```\\n\\nThe preview shows a green square. The example PNG contains one green pixel. The HTML displays it at **64 \xd7 64** so you can see it. If you see a broken-image icon, check that the comma and the start and end of the encoded data all made it inside the quotes. [Troubleshoot missing images in an HTML preview](/blog/html-preview-missing-css-images-javascript)"},{"id":"change-all-caps-website-text","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/change-all-caps-website-text","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-20-heading-in-all-caps/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-20-heading-in-all-caps/index.md","title":"How to Change All Caps to Sentence Case on a Website","description":"Fix a heading typed in all caps, or one that CSS displays in uppercase. Try both cases with a short HTML example and check the saved result.","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"HTML","permalink":"/blog/tags/html"},{"inline":true,"label":"CSS","permalink":"/blog/tags/css"}],"readingTime":2.09,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null,"socials":{}}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"change-all-caps-website-text","title":"How to Change All Caps to Sentence Case on a Website","description":"Fix a heading typed in all caps, or one that CSS displays in uppercase. Try both cases with a short HTML example and check the saved result.","image":"/tutorials/media/case-heading-css.png","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","authors":[{"key":"charly","name":"Charly Abraham"}],"tags":["Tools","HTML","CSS"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Decode Base64 Text and Preview an Encoded Image","permalink":"/blog/decode-base64-text-and-images"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Get a Hex Color From an Image and Use It in CSS","permalink":"/blog/get-hex-color-from-image"}},"content":"You change a heading to \u201cMeet at the NASA exhibit,\u201d save the file, and the page still shows **MEET AT THE NASA EXHIBIT**. Open the HTML and look between the heading tags. If the words are uppercase there, edit the text. If they already look right there, check the CSS.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## When the letters in the file are all capitals\\n\\n1. Open the [Phoenix Case Converter](https://phcode.dev/tools/case-converter.html). Paste this example into **Your text**.\\n\\n   ```text\\n   MEET AT THE NASA EXHIBIT. BRING A MAP.\\n   ```\\n\\n2. Under **For writing**, click **Sentence case**. The result is **Meet at the NASA exhibit. Bring a map.** If you choose **lowercase** instead, you get **meet at the nasa exhibit. bring a map.** That choice also changes the acronym.\\n3. Read through names, initials, and brand spellings before copying. Click **Copy**, replace only the words between the heading\u2019s opening and closing tags, and save your HTML file. For example, the first sentence could become `<h1>Meet at the NASA exhibit</h1>`.\\n\\n## When the HTML looks right but the page is still uppercase\\n\\nPaste this example into **HTML source** in the [Phoenix HTML Viewer](https://phcode.dev/tools/html-viewer.html). Choose **Preview** if the source and preview appear as tabs.\\n\\n```html\\n<style>\\n  h1 { text-transform: uppercase; }\\n</style>\\n<h1>Meet at the NASA exhibit</h1>\\n```\\n\\nThe heading in the preview reads **MEET AT THE NASA EXHIBIT**. The letters between the `<h1>` tags still have their original spelling.\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/case-heading-css.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/case-heading-css.png\\" width=\\"1032\\" height=\\"265\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"The HTML source mixes uppercase and lowercase letters in Meet at the NASA exhibit. The live preview displays the entire heading in capitals.\\" /></a>\\n\\nChange `text-transform: uppercase;` to `text-transform: none;`. The preview now reads **Meet at the NASA exhibit**. The `none` value lets the text display as you typed it, including the capitals in NASA. [CSS text-transform](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/text-transform)\\n\\nOn your own site, look for `text-transform` in the stylesheet that applies to the heading. If the file contains several matches, check the selector before editing. The example\u2019s `h1` rule affects every first-level heading. To change only this one, give it a class such as `exhibit-title` and add `.exhibit-title { text-transform: none; }` to the stylesheet. Save, reload the page, and check another heading as well. [Find text across project files in Phoenix Code](/docs/Features/find-in-files)"},{"id":"get-hex-color-from-image","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/get-hex-color-from-image","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-20-image-color-to-css/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-20-image-color-to-css/index.md","title":"How to Get a Hex Color From an Image and Use It in CSS","description":"Pick an exact pixel from a logo, copy its hex code, and test two text colors against it. Follow along with a downloadable trail marker.","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"CSS","permalink":"/blog/tags/css"}],"readingTime":2.83,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null,"socials":{}}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"get-hex-color-from-image","title":"How to Get a Hex Color From an Image and Use It in CSS","description":"Pick an exact pixel from a logo, copy its hex code, and test two text colors against it. Follow along with a downloadable trail marker.","image":"/tutorials/media/marker-text-contrast.png","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","authors":[{"key":"charly","name":"Charly Abraham"}],"tags":["Tools","CSS"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Change All Caps to Sentence Case on a Website","permalink":"/blog/change-all-caps-website-text"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Convert Markdown to PDF with Page Breaks","permalink":"/blog/convert-markdown-to-pdf-with-page-breaks"}},"content":"Suppose you want a label on your website to match the orange on a trail sign. Which orange should you copy, and will white lettering be readable on it? You can answer both questions using the picture itself. In this example the orange is `#FF8C42`. Dark text on it measures **7.75:1** for contrast.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Pick one pixel from the picture\\n\\n1. Save the <a href=\\"/tutorials/trail-marker/trail-marker.webp\\" download=\\"trail-marker.webp\\">practice trail marker</a>, or use your own logo. Open the [Phoenix Image Color Picker](https://phcode.dev/tools/image-color-picker.html), click **Choose image**, and select the file.\\n2. Click a flat patch of orange inside the sign. **Pixel magnifier** shows the spot you picked. An edge pixel can be partly transparent, so move a little farther inside if you want the solid color.\\n3. To pick the exact practice pixel, go to **Source coordinates**, enter **60** in **X** and **80** in **Y**, and click **Sample pixel**. The coordinates count from the top-left corner of the original image.\\n4. Under **Selected pixel values**, find **HEX / HEXA**. Click the copy icon beside it. For the marker, you should get `#FF8C42`. If you sample X **5**, Y **5** instead, you get `#00000000`. The final `00` means that corner is fully transparent. Return to **60**, **80** before copying the orange.\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/image-pixel-coordinates.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/image-pixel-coordinates.png\\" width=\\"443\\" height=\\"215\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"Source coordinates in the Image Color Picker show X 60 and Y 80 above the Sample pixel controls.\\" /></a>\\n\\n## Choose the lettering\\n\\nOpen the [Phoenix Color Contrast Checker](https://phcode.dev/tools/color-contrast-checker.html). Paste `#FF8C42` into **Background** and leave both color layers at full opacity. Enter each of the following values in **Foreground** and read the **AA normal text** result.\\n\\n| Text color on the orange | Contrast ratio | Result for ordinary text |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| White, `#FFFFFF` | 2.31:1 | Does not pass |\\n| Near-black, `#171717` | 7.75:1 | Passes |\\n\\nThe usual WCAG AA minimum for ordinary text is 4.5:1. The white lettering can look crisp on a large graphic and still be hard to read on a small label. [W3C guidance on minimum contrast](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html)\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/marker-text-contrast.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/marker-text-contrast.png\\" width=\\"604\\" height=\\"575\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"The contrast checker marks AA normal text and AA large text as passing for the dark lettering on the sampled orange background.\\" /></a>\\n\\n## Apply the two colors to a label\\n\\nIn your page\u2019s HTML, put `<span class=\\"trail-label\\">Woodland loop</span>` where you want the label. Add this rule to the stylesheet the page uses, then save and open the page in Live Preview.\\n\\n```css\\n.trail-label {\\n  color: #171717;\\n  background-color: #ff8c42;\\n  padding: 0.25em 0.5em;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nYou should see dark lettering on the same orange you picked from the marker. If your real label sits on a photo or uses a translucent overlay, sample and check the background behind the letters as well. [Editing colors in Phoenix Code](/docs/editing-colors)\\n\\nIf the logo is an SVG, you can [recolor one shape in its source](/blog/change-svg-fill-and-stroke). If an upload form asks for a JPG, you can [replace the transparent corners with your page\u2019s background color](/blog/convert-webp-to-jpg-background)."},{"id":"convert-markdown-to-pdf-with-page-breaks","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/convert-markdown-to-pdf-with-page-breaks","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-20-markdown-pdf-page-breaks/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-20-markdown-pdf-page-breaks/index.md","title":"How to Convert Markdown to PDF with Page Breaks","description":"Convert a Markdown README to a two-page PDF in your browser. Try the sample file, add a page break, and check that the table and code survive export.","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"Markdown","permalink":"/blog/tags/markdown"},{"inline":true,"label":"PDF","permalink":"/blog/tags/pdf"}],"readingTime":2.79,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null,"socials":{}}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"convert-markdown-to-pdf-with-page-breaks","title":"How to Convert Markdown to PDF with Page Breaks","description":"Convert a Markdown README to a two-page PDF in your browser. Try the sample file, add a page break, and check that the table and code survive export.","image":"/tutorials/markdown-pdf/converter-preview.png","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","authors":[{"key":"charly","name":"Charly Abraham"}],"tags":["Tools","Markdown","PDF"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Get a Hex Color From an Image and Use It in CSS","permalink":"/blog/get-hex-color-from-image"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Open an HTML File in Chrome","permalink":"/blog/open-an-html-file-in-chrome"}},"content":"To convert a Markdown file to PDF, open it in the [Phoenix Code Markdown to PDF Converter](https://phcode.dev/tools/markdown-to-pdf.html), choose **Save as PDF**, then save from your browser\u2019s print dialog. If you want a section to start on a new page, you can mark that spot in the Markdown.\\n\\nTry it with a short project README. The finished handout has a file table and a CSS example on page one, followed by a checklist on page two. You can do this in a browser without installing an editor or extension.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Open the README and check the preview\\n\\n1. Save <a href=\\"/tutorials/markdown-pdf/trail-notes.md\\" download=\\"trail-notes.md\\">the example Markdown file</a>, or use a `.md` file of your own.\\n2. Open the [converter](https://phcode.dev/tools/markdown-to-pdf.html), click **Open file**, and select your file. If you already have the text on your clipboard, paste it into **Markdown source**.\\n3. Under **PDF page preview**, look for the heading **Trail Notes release handoff**, a two-row table, and the CSS rule for `.route-label`. On a narrow screen, tap **Preview** to switch from the source to the rendered page.\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/markdown-pdf/converter-preview.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/markdown-pdf/converter-preview.png\\" width=\\"1175\\" height=\\"650\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"Phoenix Code shows the README beside its rendered table, CSS rule, and checklist. The toolbar shows A4 paper, Normal margins, Page numbers, and Save as PDF.\\" /></a>\\n\\n## Start the checklist on a new page\\n\\nThe sample already contains a page break. For your own README, place `\x3c!-- pagebreak --\x3e` on a line by itself just before the heading you want on the next page. Leave a blank line above and below the marker. Put it outside any fenced code block so the converter reads it as an instruction.\\n\\n```markdown\\n\x3c!-- pagebreak --\x3e\\n\\n## Before you publish\\n\\n1. Open the home page.\\n2. Check the route label and the weekend walk link.\\n3. Save a copy of this handout.\\n```\\n\\nIn the converter preview, **Before you publish** still appears just below the CSS example. The print dialog shows where one sheet ends and the next begins.\\n\\n## Save and check the two-page PDF\\n\\n1. In the converter toolbar, set **Paper** to **A4** and **Margins** to **Normal** to match the example. For US Letter paper, choose **Letter**. If your browser shows **Page numbers**, turn it on to number the sheets.\\n2. Click **Save as PDF** in the converter. In Chrome\u2019s print dialog, choose **Save as PDF** under **Destination**. For the sample, check that the print preview shows two pages.\\n3. Click **Save**, pick a folder, and name the file `trail-notes.pdf`. Open the saved PDF. On page one, search for `#256347` or select the CSS rule with your mouse. Page two should begin with **Before you publish**.\\n\\nYou can check your result against the <a href=\\"/tutorials/markdown-pdf/trail-notes.pdf\\">PDF saved from the example</a>. If the checklist stayed on page one, look for a code block above the marker that still needs its closing three backticks. If your PDF includes the converter\u2019s toolbar, use the converter\u2019s **Save as PDF** button to print the document.\\n\\nIf you want to keep editing the README in Phoenix Code, open the original `.md` file there. [Editing Markdown with Live Preview](/docs/Pro%20Features/markdown-editor)."},{"id":"open-an-html-file-in-chrome","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/open-an-html-file-in-chrome","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-20-open-html-file-in-chrome/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-20-open-html-file-in-chrome/index.md","title":"How to Open an HTML File in Chrome","description":"Open a local HTML file in desktop Chrome, check its images and styles, view the source, and reload a saved edit. Try it with a small practice website.","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"HTML","permalink":"/blog/tags/html"}],"readingTime":2.98,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null,"socials":{}}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"open-an-html-file-in-chrome","title":"How to Open an HTML File in Chrome","description":"Open a local HTML file in desktop Chrome, check its images and styles, view the source, and reload a saved edit. Try it with a small practice website.","image":"/tutorials/media/chrome-local-html.png","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","authors":[{"key":"charly","name":"Charly Abraham"}],"tags":["Tools","HTML"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Convert Markdown to PDF with Page Breaks","permalink":"/blog/convert-markdown-to-pdf-with-page-breaks"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Test Your Website on Different Screen Sizes","permalink":"/blog/test-website-on-different-screen-sizes"}},"content":"In desktop Chrome, press **Ctrl+O** on Windows or Linux, or **Command+O** on a Mac. Select your `.html` file and click **Open**. Chrome displays the page directly from your computer, even before you publish it.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Open a page from a folder\\n\\n1. Find the HTML file you want to open. To follow along, download the <a href=\\"/tutorials/first-website.zip\\" download=\\"first-website.zip\\">Trail Notes practice website</a> and extract the ZIP. Open the extracted `first-website` folder. Keep `index.html`, `styles.css`, and the `images` folder together.\\n2. Click the **+** beside the current Chrome tab to open a new tab. Use **Ctrl+O** or **Command+O** to open the file picker. Browse to the extracted folder, select **index.html**, then click **Open**.\\n3. Look for **A little time outside.** above an illustration of a winding trail. The address bar shows **File** and a path ending in `first-website/index.html`. Click **About** to open the second page, **Small walks. Good stories.** Use Chrome\u2019s back arrow to return.\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/chrome-local-html.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/chrome-local-html.png\\" width=\\"1004\\" height=\\"860\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"Chrome labels the address File and shows first-website/index.html. The Trail Notes home page has a green heading, an About link, and an illustration of a winding trail.\\" /></a>\\n\\n## See the HTML and check a saved change\\n\\nTo read the tags behind the page, press **Ctrl+U** on Windows or Linux, or **Command+Option+U** on a Mac. Chrome opens a second tab whose address starts with `view-source:`. In the sample, the line containing `href=\\"styles.css\\"` tells Chrome which stylesheet to load. [Chrome keyboard shortcuts](https://support.google.com/chrome/answer/157179?hl=en)\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/chrome-local-html-source.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/chrome-local-html-source.png\\" width=\\"1000\\" height=\\"248\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"The Chrome source tab shows view-source: before the local file path. Below it, the HTML head links to styles.css.\\" /></a>\\n\\nTo change the heading, open the same `index.html` in a text editor such as Phoenix Code. Find `<h1>A little time outside.</h1>`, change the words between the tags to `My weekend walks.`, and save the file. Return to the **Home | Trail Notes** tab and click **Reload**, the circular arrow beside the address bar. The new heading should appear.\\n\\nOnce the page looks right on your computer, check a phone width too. [Test your website on different screen sizes](/blog/test-website-on-different-screen-sizes)\\n\\n## Why do I see tags or a missing image?\\n\\n- **The entire tab shows HTML tags.** If the address starts with `view-source:`, switch back to the page tab. If the filename ends in `.html.txt`, Chrome is opening a text file. Save the HTML as plain text named `index.html` in your editor, then open that file in Chrome.\\n- **The heading appears, but the page looks plain or the trail is missing.** Check that you extracted the whole ZIP. For this sample, `styles.css` belongs beside `index.html`, and `trail.svg` belongs inside `images`.\\n\\n## Can I preview just one HTML file online?\\n\\nYes. Open the [Phoenix HTML Viewer](https://phcode.dev/tools/html-viewer.html), click **Open .html file**, and select your file. If you see **Source** and **Preview** tabs, select **Preview**. The Viewer reads the chosen document. With the Trail Notes sample, it shows the heading, while the separate stylesheet and illustration stay in your project folder. To see the complete sample, open the local `index.html` directly in Chrome. [Troubleshoot missing CSS, images, or JavaScript in the Viewer](/blog/html-preview-missing-css-images-javascript)"},{"id":"test-website-on-different-screen-sizes","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/test-website-on-different-screen-sizes","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-20-responsive-screen-sizes/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-20-responsive-screen-sizes/index.md","title":"How to Test Your Website on Different Screen Sizes","description":"Preview one HTML page at desktop, tablet, and phone widths. Find the card that makes the phone scroll sideways, fix its CSS, and check all three sizes again.","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"HTML","permalink":"/blog/tags/html"},{"inline":true,"label":"CSS","permalink":"/blog/tags/css"}],"readingTime":3,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null,"socials":{}}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"test-website-on-different-screen-sizes","title":"How to Test Your Website on Different Screen Sizes","description":"Preview one HTML page at desktop, tablet, and phone widths. Find the card that makes the phone scroll sideways, fix its CSS, and check all three sizes again.","image":"/tutorials/media/responsive-card-mobile-fit.png","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","authors":[{"key":"charly","name":"Charly Abraham"}],"tags":["Tools","HTML","CSS"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Open an HTML File in Chrome","permalink":"/blog/open-an-html-file-in-chrome"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Convert WebP to JPG and Choose the Background Color","permalink":"/blog/convert-webp-to-jpg-background"}},"content":"The **Woodland loop** card below fits on a laptop and a tablet. On a phone, the distance disappears off the right edge. Try the three screen widths in the free Phoenix HTML Viewer, then add one line of CSS to bring **2 miles** back into view.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Open the same page at three widths\\n\\n1. Open the [Phoenix HTML Viewer](https://phcode.dev/tools/html-viewer.html). Paste the whole example below into **HTML source**, replacing any text already there. If you see separate **Source** and **Preview** tabs, paste into **Source** and switch to **Preview**.\\n2. Under **Rendered preview**, open the screen-width menu. It initially reads **Responsive**. Select **Desktop**, then **Tablet**, then **Mobile**. Look for **2 miles** on the right of the green card each time.\\n\\n```html\\n<!doctype html>\\n<html lang=\\"en\\">\\n<head>\\n  <meta name=\\"viewport\\" content=\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\">\\n  <title>Weekend walks</title>\\n  <style>\\n    body { margin: 0; font: 18px/1.5 system-ui, sans-serif; }\\n    .walk {\\n      box-sizing: border-box;\\n      width: 720px;\\n      padding: 24px;\\n      color: #173c32;\\n      background: #e5f2e8;\\n    }\\n    .distance { float: right; }\\n  </style>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <main class=\\"walk\\">\\n    <strong class=\\"distance\\">2 miles</strong>\\n    <h1>Woodland loop</h1>\\n    <p>Start at the old oak and follow the green signs.</p>\\n  </main>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n```\\n\\n| Viewport preset | What you should see |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Desktop, 1280 pixels | The full green card and some white space to its right. |\\n| Tablet, 768 pixels | The full card, with a much smaller gap on the right. |\\n| Mobile, 390 pixels | The heading is visible. You have to scroll sideways inside the preview to reach **2 miles**. |\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/responsive-card-mobile-overflow.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/responsive-card-mobile-overflow.png\\" width=\\"692\\" height=\\"350\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"Mobile is selected in the Phoenix HTML Viewer. The Woodland loop heading is visible, but the distance lies outside the right edge of the phone preview.\\" /></a>\\n\\n## Fix the card and test again\\n\\nIn **HTML source**, find `width: 720px;` inside the `.walk` rule. Keep that line and add the following one immediately below it.\\n\\n```css\\nmax-width: 100%;\\n```\\n\\nSwitch back to **Preview** if needed. At **Mobile**, the distance and heading should now be visible together. The description wraps to a second line. Switch back to **Tablet** and **Desktop**. The card still stops at 720 pixels on those wider screens.\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/responsive-card-mobile-fit.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/responsive-card-mobile-fit.png\\" width=\\"692\\" height=\\"350\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"After adding max-width, the Mobile preview shows both Woodland loop and 2 miles inside the green card. The description wraps within its right edge.\\" /></a>\\n\\nThe card needed 720 pixels even when its parent had only 390. The new maximum lets it shrink to the available width. The existing `box-sizing: border-box` keeps the padding inside that width. On your own page, apply the change to the element that sticks out, save the stylesheet, and repeat the same width checks. [How CSS max-width works](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/max-width)\\n\\n## Test a project with separate files\\n\\nIf your HTML links to a local stylesheet or images, open the whole project in Phoenix Code so Live Preview can find those files. In **Phoenix Pro**, the device-size button at the right of the Live Preview toolbar lets you switch widths. After checking the layout, open your published page on a real phone to try the links, touch controls, and on-screen keyboard. [Device Preview in Phoenix Pro](/docs/Pro%20Features/device-preview) \xb7 [Troubleshoot missing styles and images](/blog/html-preview-missing-css-images-javascript)"},{"id":"convert-webp-to-jpg-background","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/convert-webp-to-jpg-background","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-20-webp-to-jpg-background/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-20-webp-to-jpg-background/index.md","title":"How to Convert WebP to JPG and Choose the Background Color","description":"Convert a transparent WebP to a JPG with a white or custom background. Compare two actual downloads and see when to keep a transparent PNG instead.","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"Images","permalink":"/blog/tags/images"}],"readingTime":2.41,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null,"socials":{}}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"convert-webp-to-jpg-background","title":"How to Convert WebP to JPG and Choose the Background Color","description":"Convert a transparent WebP to a JPG with a white or custom background. Compare two actual downloads and see when to keep a transparent PNG instead.","image":"/tutorials/media/webp-jpg-download.png","date":"2026-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","authors":[{"key":"charly","name":"Charly Abraham"}],"tags":["Tools","Images"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Test Your Website on Different Screen Sizes","permalink":"/blog/test-website-on-different-screen-sizes"},"nextItem":{"title":"Why Your HTML Preview Is Missing CSS, Images or JavaScript","permalink":"/blog/html-preview-missing-css-images-javascript"}},"content":"A logo can float over any page color as a transparent WebP. A JPG needs a color in every pixel. If an upload form requires JPG, choose the background **before adding the file**. These two images came from the same WebP. Only the background setting changed.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n| White background | Dark green background |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| <img src=\\"/tutorials/trail-marker/marker-white.jpg\\" width=\\"240\\" height=\\"160\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} alt=\\"The orange trail marker on a white rectangular background.\\" /> | <img src=\\"/tutorials/trail-marker/marker-dark.jpg\\" width=\\"240\\" height=\\"160\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} alt=\\"The same orange trail marker on a dark green rectangular background.\\" /> |\\n\\n## Download a JPG with the background you want\\n\\n1. Open the [Phoenix WebP to JPG Converter](https://phcode.dev/tools/webp-to-jpg.html). Use your own WebP, or download the <a href=\\"/tutorials/trail-marker/trail-marker.webp\\" download=\\"trail-marker.webp\\">transparent trail marker</a> to follow along.\\n2. Leave **Image quality** at **90%** for this example. Under **Background color**, keep white (`#FFFFFF`) for the first result, or click the color swatch and choose the color you want behind the image. We used dark green (`#21392F`) for the second version.\\n3. Click **Choose files** and select the WebP. Conversion starts when you select it. In **Your JPG files**, use the download button on the row for your image.\\n4. Open the downloaded `trail-marker.jpg`. The marker still measures **240 \xd7 160 pixels**. Look at the four corners to see the chosen background. If you want another version, set the new color and add the original WebP again.\\n\\n<a href=\\"/tutorials/media/webp-jpg-download.png\\"><img src=\\"/tutorials/media/webp-jpg-download.png\\" width=\\"1092\\" height=\\"300\\" style={{height: \'auto\'}} loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"The converted trail-marker.jpg is ready under Your JPG files, with its download control beside the file.\\" /></a>\\n\\n## Why does the white box stay?\\n\\nCheck which file you added. If you start the second conversion with the **white JPG**, the white corners are already part of the picture. Go back to the original transparent WebP. The background setting fills transparent pixels. It leaves an opaque white area in a photo or logo in place.\\n\\nThe quality slider controls JPG compression. Increasing it won\u2019t restore transparency, and decreasing it can soften the sharp edges of lettering. Compare the downloaded file at the size visitors will see it. [Image formats and transparency](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Media/Guides/Formats/Image_types)\\n\\n## Keep the transparent corners\\n\\nIf the same logo must sit on both light and dark pages, keep the original WebP or open it in the [Phoenix WebP to PNG Converter](https://phcode.dev/tools/webp-to-png.html). Choose the original file and download the PNG. In our practice export, the corner pixel stayed fully transparent. You can check your own file by sampling a corner in the [image color picker walkthrough](/blog/get-hex-color-from-image#pick-one-pixel-from-the-picture)."},{"id":"html-preview-missing-css-images-javascript","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/html-preview-missing-css-images-javascript","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-19-html-preview-missing-assets/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-19-html-preview-missing-assets/index.md","title":"Why Your HTML Preview Is Missing CSS, Images or JavaScript","description":"Fix CSS not loading, missing images and JavaScript that won\'t run in an HTML preview. Check file paths and permissions with working examples.","date":"2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"HTML","permalink":"/blog/tags/html"},{"inline":true,"label":"CSS","permalink":"/blog/tags/css"},{"inline":true,"label":"JavaScript","permalink":"/blog/tags/java-script"}],"readingTime":13.3,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"html-preview-missing-css-images-javascript","title":"Why Your HTML Preview Is Missing CSS, Images or JavaScript","description":"Fix CSS not loading, missing images and JavaScript that won\'t run in an HTML preview. Check file paths and permissions with working examples.","image":"/tutorials/media/html-preview-hosted-result.png","date":"2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z","authors":["charly"],"tags":["Tools","HTML","CSS","JavaScript"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Convert WebP to JPG and Choose the Background Color","permalink":"/blog/convert-webp-to-jpg-background"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Convert PNG to PDF for Free Online","permalink":"/blog/how-to-convert-png-to-pdf-free-online"}},"content":"import TutorialIcon from \'@site/src/components/TutorialIcon\';\\nimport HtmlPreviewVideo from \'@site/src/components/Video/HtmlPreviewVideo\';\\nimport \'@site/src/css/html-preview-guide.css\';\\n\\nAn HTML file can link to CSS, images, and JavaScript without copying those files\\ninto it. If a preview is missing styles or images, first check the file paths and\\nthe preview permissions. If a button does nothing, check whether scripts are allowed.\\nThis guide also helps when CSS is not loading, HTML images are not showing, or\\nJavaScript is not working in HTML.\\n\\nThe examples below use [Phoenix HTML Viewer](https://phcode.dev/tools/html-viewer.html).\\nHTML and CSS written inside the document work by default. Hosted files and\\nJavaScript each need permission in the Viewer. The Viewer reads the HTML file you open;\\nit does not import the files beside it.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n**You will need:** your HTML text or an `.html` file. The Viewer is free and runs\\nin your browser. For the last steps, keep any separate CSS, JavaScript, and image\\nfiles with your HTML. You can use file editing and Live Preview. These features\\nare in both Phoenix Pro and the free Community edition.\\n\\n## Identify what is missing\\n\\n| What you see | First check | Next step |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Hosted CSS is not loading | Is external access enabled? | For trusted content, turn on **Allow external resources**. Then [try the hosted example](#distinguish-hosted-addresses-from-relative-paths). |\\n| A local `styles.css` file is not loading | Did you open only the HTML file? | [Open the full project folder](#open-the-complete-project-in-phoenix-code) in Phoenix Code. |\\n| An image is not showing | Is its address a hosted URL or a local path? | [Check the path and filename](#keep-local-files-in-the-expected-folders). |\\n| A hosted file still does not load | What URL did the browser request? | [Check the failed request](#check-a-failed-resource). |\\n| A button does nothing | Is JavaScript allowed? | [Test a small inline script](#check-a-small-javascript-interaction) before you debug a bigger one. |\\n\\nIf the stylesheet loads but the page still looks wrong, check whether the CSS\\nrule targets the right element or another rule overrides it. For a heading that\\nstays in capitals, [compare the HTML text with its CSS text-transform rule](/blog/change-all-caps-website-text#when-the-html-looks-right-but-the-page-is-still-uppercase).\\n\\n## Test HTML and inline CSS {#check-a-self-contained-html-example}\\n\\n1. Open the [HTML Viewer](https://phcode.dev/tools/html-viewer.html).\\n2. Click inside **HTML source** and paste the complete document below, replacing\\n   any sample text already there. It needs no downloaded stylesheet, image, or\\n   JavaScript.\\n\\n```html\\n<!doctype html>\\n<html lang=\'en\'>\\n<head>\\n  <meta charset=\'utf-8\'>\\n  <meta name=\'viewport\' content=\'width=device-width, initial-scale=1\'>\\n  <title>Preview check</title>\\n  <style>\\n    body {\\n      background: #f4f6f8;\\n      padding: 24px;\\n    }\\n    p {\\n      color: #164ea6;\\n      font-size: 20px;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <p>HTML and inline CSS are working.</p>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n```\\n\\n3. Look at the **Rendered preview** panel. On a narrow screen, switch from **Source** to\\n   **Preview** to see the result.\\n\\nYou should see a blue paragraph on a pale background.\\n\\n<figure className=\\"html-preview-figure\\">\\n  <a href=\\"/tutorials/media/html-preview-inline-result.png\\">\\n    <img src=\\"/tutorials/media/html-preview-inline-result.png\\" width=\\"1250\\" height=\\"670\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"HTML Viewer showing the example\'s blue paragraph on a pale background, with Allow external resources unchecked.\\" />\\n  </a>\\n  <figcaption>The complete example works with external resources off. Select the image to inspect the source and preview it at full size.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\nIf this works, add parts of your original page a little at a time: HTML first,\\nthen styles, images, and scripts. When the preview breaks, check the last part\\nyou added.\\n\\nTo check your own file, choose **Open .html file** above the source editor. This\\nreplaces the current source. To keep your edits, use **Download .html** in the\\nsource toolbar first. On desktop, this is the download-arrow button with that tooltip.\\n\\nTo see a saved page with the CSS and images beside it on your computer, [open the local HTML file in Chrome](/blog/open-an-html-file-in-chrome).\\n\\nIf changes stop appearing, check whether **Live preview** is on. Its checkbox\\nand **Allow external resources** are above the editor on a wide screen. On a\\nnarrow screen, open **Preview settings** below the workspace. When **Live\\npreview** is off, use **Refresh preview** after you edit.\\n\\n<figure className=\\"html-preview-figure html-preview-mobile-figure\\">\\n  <a href=\\"/tutorials/media/html-preview-mobile-settings.png\\">\\n    <img src=\\"/tutorials/media/html-preview-mobile-settings.png\\" width=\\"322\\" height=\\"278\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"Expanded mobile Preview settings showing Live preview and Allow external resources checked, with Run scripts unchecked.\\" />\\n  </a>\\n  <figcaption>On mobile, open <strong>Preview settings</strong>. There, external resources are on and scripts stay off.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## Keep local files in the expected folders\\n\\nFor a working example, download the [image-and-text practice project](/tutorials/image-text-layout.zip)\\nused in our [image layout tutorial](/docs/tutorials/image-text-layout). Extract the\\nZIP first. Keep these files together in the extracted folder:\\n\\n```text\\nimage-text-layout/\\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 index.html\\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 styles.css\\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 images/\\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 woodland.jpg\\n```\\n\\nInside `index.html`, a stylesheet reference belongs in the `head`:\\n\\n```html\\n<link rel=\'stylesheet\' href=\'styles.css\'>\\n```\\n\\nAn image reference belongs where the image should appear in the body:\\n\\n```html\\n<img src=\'images/woodland.jpg\' alt=\'A sunlit path between tall trees\'\\n  width=\'1200\' height=\'675\'>\\n```\\n\\nThe first path expects `styles.css` beside the HTML file. The second expects\\n`woodland.jpg` inside an `images` folder. Check that the\\nfilenames and case match the files you have. For your own project,\\nuse your image\'s filename, dimensions, and description.\\n\\nOpening only `index.html` in the Viewer leaves those files behind.\\n**Allow external resources** cannot give the Viewer access to other files on\\nyour computer. Keep the files together and use\\nthe [full-project workflow below](#open-the-complete-project-in-phoenix-code).\\n\\nIf the missing image is the small icon in the browser tab, follow [Add a favicon in HTML](/blog/how-to-add-a-favicon-in-html) to check it on both the home page and a nested page.\\n\\n## Check hosted URLs and relative paths {#distinguish-hosted-addresses-from-relative-paths}\\n\\nA full URL such as `https://example.com/images/logo.png` points to a file on a\\nwebsite. This is an example URL; replace it with your own image URL when testing.\\nHosted images, stylesheets, and fonts need **Allow external resources** in the Viewer.\\n\\nAn image address that starts with `data:image/` carries the image inside the HTML. If that image is missing, [test the complete data URL with a working Base64 image](/blog/decode-base64-text-and-images#see-an-image-copied-from-html).\\n\\nTo see the difference, replace the Viewer source with this complete example.\\nIt loads the same practice stylesheet and woodland photo from the docs website:\\n\\n```html\\n<!doctype html>\\n<html lang=\'en\'>\\n<head>\\n  <meta charset=\'utf-8\'>\\n  <meta name=\'viewport\' content=\'width=device-width, initial-scale=1\'>\\n  <title>Hosted resource check</title>\\n  <link rel=\'stylesheet\'\\n    href=\'https://docs.phcode.dev/tutorials/image-text-layout/styles.css\'>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <h1>A woodland walk</h1>\\n  <div class=\'feature\'>\\n    <img\\n      src=\'https://docs.phcode.dev/tutorials/image-text-layout/images/woodland.jpg\'\\n      alt=\'A sunlit path between tall trees\' width=\'320\' height=\'180\'>\\n  </div>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n```\\n\\n1. With **Allow external resources** off, the heading is unstyled and the photo\\n   does not load.\\n2. Turn on **Allow external resources** for this example. You should see a dark\\n   green heading, a pale background, and the woodland photo. Leave scripts off;\\n   this example needs only HTML, CSS, and an image.\\n\\n<figure className=\\"html-preview-figure html-preview-video\\">\\n  <HtmlPreviewVideo topic=\\"hosted\\" showWatchLink />\\n  <figcaption>Someone has already pasted the example into the Viewer. The recording shows that external access is off, someone turned on the setting, and the hosted files are loading.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\nThe photo is by [Johannes Plenio on Unsplash](https://unsplash.com/photos/pathway-of-trees-during-daytime-P1Iz3unD6Po),\\nas credited in the practice project. This example uses complete hosted addresses;\\nit does not access files on your computer.\\n\\nA relative path starts from another location. For paths in your HTML:\\n\\n- `styles.css` and `./styles.css` resolve from the current directory.\\n- `../styles.css` resolves from the parent directory.\\n- On an HTTP or HTTPS page, the browser resolves `/styles.css` from the site\\n  root, even when the page is in a subfolder.\\n\\nA relative path does not mean \'find this file anywhere on my computer.\'\\n[MDN explains how relative references are resolved](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URL_API/Resolving_relative_references).\\n\\nFor a background image in an external CSS file, a relative `url(...)` starts\\nfrom [the stylesheet\'s location](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/url_function),\\nwhich may be a different folder from the HTML file.\\n\\n### If a hosted file still will not load {#check-a-failed-resource}\\n\\nPermission alone will not fix a wrong URL or a missing file. Open the image or\\nstylesheet URL in a new tab. It should show the image or CSS itself, not a sign-in screen or a sharing page.\\n\\nTo see what the preview actually requested in Chrome on desktop:\\n\\n1. Open Chrome\'s three-dot menu, then **More tools > Developer tools**. Select\\n   **Network** in the developer tools panel.\\n2. In the Viewer, turn **Live preview** off and click **Refresh preview**. Keep\\n   **Allow external resources** on only for content you trust.\\n3. Find the image or CSS request in **Network**. Select it and open **Headers**\\n   to check **Request URL** and **Status Code**.\\n\\n| What you find | What to check |\\n|---|---|\\n| `404` | The server could not find that URL. Check the filename, case, and folder path. |\\n| A blocked request | Read the browser\'s **Console** message for the reason. Renaming the file will not fix a preview restriction. |\\n| `200`, but the page is still unstyled | Check that the response contains the expected CSS, then check which rules apply to the element. |\\n\\n[Chrome\'s Network guide](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/network)\\nexplains the request details. Turn **Live preview** back on when you are done.\\nExternal resources can contact other websites, so leave them off for HTML you\\ndo not trust. Do not disable browser security to make a preview work.\\n\\n## Test a button with inline JavaScript {#check-a-small-javascript-interaction}\\n\\nPaste the [first, self-contained example](#check-a-self-contained-html-example)\\nback into the Viewer. Add this button after the paragraph, just before the\\nclosing `</body>` tag. Start with **Allow external resources** off.\\n\\n```html\\n<button type=\'button\' onclick=\'this.textContent=`Clicked`\'>\\n  Test button\\n</button>\\n```\\n\\nWith scripts off, the button appears, but clicking it does not change its label.\\nTurn on **Allow external resources**, then select **Run scripts \u2014\\nenable external resources first**. Click the **Test button** in the preview again;\\nits label should change to **Clicked**.\\n\\n<figure className=\\"html-preview-figure html-preview-video\\">\\n  <HtmlPreviewVideo topic=\\"scripts\\" showWatchLink />\\n  <figcaption>The button is already in the source. Its first click does nothing. After you enable both settings, click it again and the label changes to <strong>Clicked</strong>.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\nChanging these permission settings refreshes the preview and resets the button.\\nClick it again after you change a setting.\\n\\nThis tests JavaScript written inside the HTML. In pasted HTML, the Viewer can\\nrun inline scripts, but it blocks external `<script src>` files and `fetch`/XHR\\nrequests.\\n\\nTo test a small example with a separate stylesheet or script, open the\\n[online HTML editor](https://phcode.dev/tools/html-editor.html). Paste your HTML,\\nCSS and JavaScript into the `index.html`, `style.css` and `script.js` tabs.\\n\\nFor a project with more files or modules, open the full folder in an editor.\\nIf it also needs a backend server, run that server too.\\n\\nIf your own inline script still fails, open the developer tools **Console**.\\nFix the first error from your script, then test again.\\n\\n## Open the complete project in Phoenix Code\\n\\nFor a complete editing workspace, use [Phoenix Code](https://phcode.dev/).\\nWork on your HTML, CSS and JavaScript files together, with Live Preview alongside\\nyour code.\\n\\nFor HTML that links to local CSS, images, or scripts, use\\n[Phoenix Code\'s project workflow](/docs/quick-start-project#open-folder).\\nFollow the extracted practice project above. Open its original\\n`index.html`, which uses local paths. The hosted example above was only for\\ntesting resource permissions.\\n\\nFor your own project:\\n\\n1. If you edited the document in the Viewer, use **Download .html** to save it.\\n   This downloads the HTML source; it does not collect referenced images or\\n   stylesheets.\\n2. Keep that HTML file with its CSS, JavaScript, and images in the folders its\\n   paths point to.\\n3. Open [Phoenix Code](https://phcode.dev/) on desktop, or use [its browser app](https://web.phcode.dev)\\n   in Chrome or Edge for local-folder access.\\n4. In the **Start Project Dialog**, choose **Open Folder** and select the folder\\n   containing the HTML file. If the dialog is closed, click the folder icon at\\n   the top left to open it again. The project files should appear in the file list.\\n5. Click your HTML file in the file list. If the preview is hidden, click the\\n   <TutorialIcon name=\\"preview\\" label=\\"lightning bolt\\" /> at the top of the right\\n   toolbar to show [Live Preview](/docs/Features/Live%20Preview).\\n6. If Phoenix Code asks whether you trust the project, choose **Trust & Execute\\n   Preview** only for a project you trust.\\n7. Fix any incorrect paths in the HTML and use **File > Save File**. In Live\\n   Preview, check that the stylesheet changes the page and the\\n   image loads.\\n\\nThe Viewer link does not transfer your project. Save your edited HTML in its\\nproject folder, then open that folder in Phoenix Code.\\n\\nIn the practice project, opening `index.html` should show **Plan a woodland walk**,\\nthe forest photo, and **Take the scenic route**. To arrange that photo beside its\\ntext, continue with the [image-and-text layout tutorial](/docs/tutorials/image-text-layout).\\n\\n## Q&A\\n\\n**Question:** Why does inline CSS work in the HTML Viewer, but my separate styles.css file does not?\\n\\n**Short answer:** Inline CSS is part of the HTML document you paste or open, so the Viewer can render it right away. A separate local styles.css file is not loaded when you open only the HTML file. If your page uses local CSS, images, or JavaScript files, keep them in the right folders. Open the full project folder in Phoenix Code instead of just opening the HTML file in the Viewer.\\n\\n**Question:** Does **Allow external resources** make local files on my computer available to the preview?\\n\\n**Short answer:** No. **Allow external resources** lets the Viewer load hosted resources from websites, such as full image or stylesheet URLs. It does not give the Viewer access to other files beside your HTML on your computer. For local files like styles.css or images/woodland.jpg, use the full-project workflow and open the folder that contains all related files.\\n\\n**Question:** What should I check first when an image does not appear?\\n\\n**Short answer:** First check whether the image uses a hosted URL or a local relative path. For hosted images, make sure **Allow external resources** is enabled and that the URL opens the actual image, not a sign-in page or a sharing page. For local images, make sure the filename, case, and folder path match exactly. Remember, a relative path does not search your entire computer.\\n\\n**Question:** Why does my button show up but not respond when clicked?\\n\\n**Short answer:** The button may rely on JavaScript, and scripts are off by default in the Viewer. For trusted content, enable **Allow external resources** first, then enable **Run scripts \u2014 enable external resources first**. If inline JavaScript still fails, open the browser developer tools **Console**, fix the first script error shown there, and test again.\\n\\n**Question:** When should I switch from the HTML Viewer to Phoenix Code?\\n\\n**Short answer:** Use the HTML Viewer for quick checks with pasted HTML, inline CSS, hosted resources, or small inline JavaScript tests. Use Phoenix Code when your page needs local CSS, images, JavaScript files, modules, or a full folder structure. Phoenix Code\'s project workflow lets Live Preview resolve local paths from the project folder."},{"id":"how-to-convert-png-to-pdf-free-online","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/how-to-convert-png-to-pdf-free-online","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-08-png-to-pdf-free-online/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-08-png-to-pdf-free-online/index.md","title":"How to Convert PNG to PDF for Free Online","description":"Combine PNG images into one PDF in your browser. Arrange pages, choose paper settings and download without uploading your images or creating an account.","date":"2026-11-08T00:30:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"PDF","permalink":"/blog/tags/pdf"}],"readingTime":6.2,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"how-to-convert-png-to-pdf-free-online","title":"How to Convert PNG to PDF for Free Online","description":"Combine PNG images into one PDF in your browser. Arrange pages, choose paper settings and download without uploading your images or creating an account.","image":"/tutorials/media/png-to-pdf-convert-poster.png","date":"2026-11-08T00:30:00.000Z","authors":["charly"],"tags":["Tools","PDF"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Why Your HTML Preview Is Missing CSS, Images or JavaScript","permalink":"/blog/html-preview-missing-css-images-javascript"},"nextItem":{"title":"PNG to PDF Quality: Pixels, Color and Print Size","permalink":"/blog/how-to-convert-png-to-pdf-without-losing-quality"}},"content":"import \'@site/src/css/png-pdf-guides.css\';\\nimport PngPdfVideo from \'@site/src/components/Video/PngPdfVideo\';\\n\\n<span className=\\"png-pdf-guide-marker\\" hidden />\\n\\nYou can turn a PNG screenshot, scanned page or diagram into a PDF without installing software. The [Phoenix Code PNG to PDF converter](https://phcode.dev/tools/png-to-pdf.html) combines still PNG images into one file, with one image per page. Conversion runs locally in your browser, and the downloaded PDF has no watermark. No account or subscription is required.\\n\\nThis guide walks through choosing images, arranging pages and saving the result. For color, resolution and printing details, see [what PNG-to-PDF conversion preserves](./../2026-11-08-png-to-pdf-quality/index.md).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Watch a complete conversion\\n\\nFollow along with **Try an example** in the converter. This walkthrough drags the parrot above the coastal picture, chooses **A4** with **10 mm** margins, and opens the downloaded PDF in Chrome\'s PDF viewer. Select **Choose PNG files** when you are ready to use your own images.\\n\\n<PngPdfVideo topic=\\"convert\\" showWatchLink />\\n\\n## 1. Choose your PNG images\\n\\nOpen the [PNG to PDF converter](https://phcode.dev/tools/png-to-pdf.html), select **Choose PNG files**, and pick one or more still PNG images from your device. On a computer, you can also drop PNG files into the converter or paste PNG images from the clipboard.\\n\\nOn a phone, use the file picker to choose your images. If your phone saved a photo in another format, first obtain a PNG version: this converter does not accept every image format, and renaming a file\'s extension does not convert it.\\n\\nThe tool checks each image before it can be included. If a file has an error, read its message and remove or replace it. Every remaining image must be ready before **Create PDF** becomes available.\\n\\nDamaged or unsupported color information, including unsupported HDR data, produces a specific error. Export a copy as a standard sRGB PNG and try again, keeping your original file.\\n\\n## 2. Put the pages in order\\n\\nEach image becomes one PDF page. The order of the cards is the order of the pages in the finished file.\\n\\n- With a mouse, drag from the image preview, filename or empty space on a card.\\n- On a touch screen, press and hold a card briefly, then drag it. A normal swipe still scrolls the page.\\n- Use **Move up** and **Move down** when you prefer buttons.\\n- With a keyboard, focus a card and press Enter or Space. Choose its position with the arrow keys, Home or End, then press Enter or Space again. Escape cancels the move.\\n\\nThe card\'s action buttons retain their own actions. You can remove an unwanted image without restarting the whole batch.\\n\\n<figure className=\\"png-pdf-figure\\">\\n  <a href=\\"/tutorials/media/png-to-pdf-page-order.png\\" aria-label=\\"Open full-size screenshot of the page order controls\\">\\n    <img src=\\"/tutorials/media/png-to-pdf-page-order.png\\" width=\\"1088\\" height=\\"691\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" alt=\\"Two sample PNGs appear as Page 1 and Page 2, each with Move up, Move down and Remove buttons.\\" />\\n  </a>\\n  <figcaption>Drag the second card above the first to change the page order. Start from its preview, filename, or empty space; the buttons are separate actions.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## 3. Choose paper and background settings\\n\\nThe settings affect every page, with automatic orientation determined separately for each image when a paper size is selected.\\n\\n| Setting | What to choose |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Page size | **Fit to image** for a page based on each image\'s dimensions and stored print resolution; **A4** or **US Letter** for standard paper. |\\n| Orientation | With A4 or US Letter, choose **Automatic**, **Portrait** or **Landscape**. Fit to image determines the page shape from the image. |\\n| Margins | Choose none, 5 mm, 10 mm or 20 mm. Images fit inside the available area without cropping or stretching. |\\n| Transparent areas | Choose **White** to give transparent image areas a white background, or **Transparent** to retain transparency. |\\n\\nFit to image uses the PNG\'s stored print resolution and adds the selected margins. If no resolution is stored, it uses 96 pixels per inch. For example, a 2400-pixel-wide PNG stored at 300 pixels per inch occupies 8 inches before margins. A4 and US Letter fit the image to the chosen paper, changing its printed size while keeping the original pixel dimensions.\\n\\nStored image orientation and pixel aspect ratios are respected. If the stored resolution would make an image-sized PDF page too large or too small, choose A4 or US Letter and create the PDF again.\\n\\n## 4. Check the layout, create the PDF and save it\\n\\nThe page previews show orientation, margins and image placement. They use smaller preview images, so use them to check the layout rather than fine detail or final color.\\n\\nSelect **Create PDF** and keep the browser tab open while it works. When the PDF is ready, select **Download PDF** to save it to your device. Open that downloaded file to check page order, readability and appearance before printing or sharing it.\\n\\nEach picture is a separate page; your PDF viewer may show the pages one below another. If you keep **Fit to image**, the pages follow your images\' dimensions instead of the A4 paper shown in the video.\\n\\nChanging the page order or settings after creation requires creating the PDF again. Your original PNG files stay on your device.\\n\\n<figure className=\\"png-pdf-figure\\">\\n  <a href=\\"/tutorials/media/png-to-pdf-download-ready.png\\" aria-label=\\"Open full-size screenshot of the finished conversion\\">\\n    <img src=\\"/tutorials/media/png-to-pdf-download-ready.png\\" width=\\"1088\\" height=\\"691\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" alt=\\"The parrot is now Page 1 and the coastal picture is Page 2. A4 and 10 mm margins are selected, and Download PDF is available.\\" />\\n  </a>\\n  <figcaption>After Create PDF finishes, Download PDF saves the result. Open the saved file to check both pages.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## How many images can you combine?\\n\\nAll of these limits apply together:\\n\\n| Limit | Maximum |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Images | 100 still PNG files |\\n| Individual file | 50 MB |\\n| Total input files | 200 MB |\\n| Pixels in one image | 24 megapixels |\\n| Pixels across the batch | 48 megapixels |\\n| Either image dimension | 16,384 pixels |\\n| Finished PDF | 128 MB |\\n\\nA batch of large scans may reach the pixel or output limit before it reaches 100 images. Available device memory can also limit a conversion. If a batch fails, try fewer or smaller images and keep the tab open.\\n\\n## What happens to your files?\\n\\nThe tool uses browser memory to process the images and generate the PDF. It does not upload your images or PDF to a conversion server or save them in persistent browser storage. The page still loads website code and other assets.\\n\\nYour original PNG files and downloaded PDF remain on your device. Closing the tab does not delete them. If you are sharing a sensitive document, check the contents of the downloaded PDF and the destination before sending it.\\n\\n## Can the PDF text be searched or copied?\\n\\nThis tool creates image-based PDF pages. Text in a screenshot remains part of the image; the converter does not perform optical character recognition (OCR). Use an OCR tool if you need a searchable or selectable text layer.\\n\\nWhen your images are ready, [open the PNG to PDF converter](https://phcode.dev/tools/png-to-pdf.html) and create your file. If you plan to print it, first read [how pixel dimensions, color and paper size affect the result](./../2026-11-08-png-to-pdf-quality/index.md)."},{"id":"how-to-convert-png-to-pdf-without-losing-quality","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/how-to-convert-png-to-pdf-without-losing-quality","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-11-08-png-to-pdf-quality/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-08-png-to-pdf-quality/index.md","title":"PNG to PDF Quality: Pixels, Color and Print Size","description":"Understand PNG-to-PDF image quality: original pixels, bit depth, color profiles, transparency, stored print resolution and paper-size settings.","date":"2026-11-08T00:30:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/blog/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"PDF","permalink":"/blog/tags/pdf"}],"readingTime":7.41,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"how-to-convert-png-to-pdf-without-losing-quality","title":"PNG to PDF Quality: Pixels, Color and Print Size","description":"Understand PNG-to-PDF image quality: original pixels, bit depth, color profiles, transparency, stored print resolution and paper-size settings.","image":"/tutorials/media/png-to-pdf-settings-poster.png","date":"2026-11-08T00:30:00.000Z","authors":["charly"],"tags":["Tools","PDF"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Convert PNG to PDF for Free Online","permalink":"/blog/how-to-convert-png-to-pdf-free-online"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phoenix Code 5.5.11: Layers, Visual AI & ChatGPT Codex","permalink":"/blog/release-5.5"}},"content":"import \'@site/src/css/png-pdf-guides.css\';\\nimport PngPdfVideo from \'@site/src/components/Video/PngPdfVideo\';\\n\\n<span className=\\"png-pdf-guide-marker\\" hidden />\\n\\nConverting a PNG to PDF can preserve image detail, but \u201cno quality loss\u201d needs a precise meaning. Pixel dimensions, color profiles, bit depth and print size are different properties. Preserving one does not guarantee that all the others stay unchanged.\\n\\nThe [Phoenix Code PNG to PDF converter](https://phcode.dev/tools/png-to-pdf.html) preserves original PNG pixel values, dimensions and bit depth, including 16-bit images. It uses lossless compression and copies supported embedded color profiles into the PDF. **Fit to image** also uses stored print resolution. PDF readers, screens and printers can still affect how colors look, so check the downloaded file and keep your source PNGs.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What the converter preserves\\n\\n| Property | Behavior in this converter |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Image pixels and dimensions | Original pixel values and dimensions are kept. A 2400 \xd7 1600 image remains 2400 \xd7 1600 pixels in the PDF. |\\n| Compression | Lossless image compression is used. Compression does not automatically mean loss of detail. |\\n| Color and bit depth | Original bit depth, including 16-bit values, is kept. Supported embedded color profiles are copied into the PDF. Colors without color information are treated as sRGB. |\\n| Transparency | Can be retained with **Transparent**, or placed on white with **White**. |\\n| Stored print resolution | Used by **Fit to image**, with a 96-pixels-per-inch fallback when no resolution is stored. Standard paper sizes change the print scale. |\\n| Stored orientation | Applied to the image\'s placement. Rotated or mirrored images keep their intended orientation. |\\n| Other image metadata | Not copied into the PDF. |\\n| Image placement | The image fits within the chosen paper and margins without cropping or stretching. |\\n| Text in screenshots | Remains an image. No searchable or editable OCR text layer is created. |\\n\\nLossless compression stores the original image samples without discarding detail. The PDF has its own structure, so its file size can be larger than the source PNG. The small browser previews remain layout aids; use the downloaded PDF to inspect fine detail and color.\\n\\nSupported RGB and grayscale ICC profiles are copied unchanged. Standard sRGB and SDR Display P3 color information are also supported. Images with no color information use an sRGB assumption. Damaged or unsupported color information, including unsupported HDR data, produces an error. If this happens, export a standard sRGB PNG copy and try again, keeping the original for any work that needs its unsupported color data.\\n\\n## Pixel dimensions and print size are different\\n\\nThe number of pixels alone does not determine how large an image prints. Its physical size also matters. Effective pixels per inch (PPI) are calculated as:\\n\\n**PPI = pixels along one side \xf7 printed inches along that side.**\\n\\nFor example, an image that is 2400 pixels wide provides:\\n\\n| Printed width | Effective resolution |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| 8 inches | 300 PPI |\\n| 12 inches | 200 PPI |\\n| 25 inches | 96 PPI |\\n\\nThese are arithmetic examples, not a guarantee that a particular image meets a printer\'s requirements. Check the required physical dimensions and resolution for the intended print job.\\n\\nChanging paper size does not create more detail. A larger placement spreads the same pixels over a larger area; a smaller placement brings them closer together.\\n\\n<figure className=\\"png-pdf-figure\\">\\n  <img src=\\"/tutorials/media/png-to-pdf-print-size.svg\\" width=\\"880\\" height=\\"408\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" alt=\\"The same 2400-pixel image provides 300 pixels per inch at 8 inches wide, or 200 pixels per inch at 12 inches wide.\\" />\\n  <figcaption>The longer line represents a wider print of the same image. Its pixel count has not increased.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## Choose a page size for the intended use\\n\\nThe walkthrough starts with the converter\'s landscape sample already added. It shows the paper, margin and transparency choices, then shows the downloaded PDF in Chrome\'s PDF viewer. To follow along, choose **Try an example** in the [converter](https://phcode.dev/tools/png-to-pdf.html) and remove the coastal picture, leaving the parrot.\\n\\n<PngPdfVideo topic=\\"settings\\" showWatchLink />\\n\\n### Fit to image\\n\\nUse **Fit to image** when you want each PDF page sized from that image\'s pixel dimensions and stored print resolution. The tool uses the PNG\'s resolution information, falling back to valid EXIF resolution and then to 96 pixels per inch when no resolution is available. Selected margins add space around the image.\\n\\nA 2400-pixel-wide image stored at 300 pixels per inch occupies 8 inches before margins. The same image without stored resolution uses 96 pixels per inch and occupies 25 inches. Horizontal and vertical resolution can differ; the converter respects both, along with stored pixel aspect ratios and orientation.\\n\\nThe resolution setting is often called DPI in image software. Keeping that setting preserves the intended print scale, while actual print quality still depends on the source image, PDF reader and printer. If the stored resolution would create a page that is too large or too small, choose A4 or US Letter and create the PDF again.\\n\\n### A4 or US Letter\\n\\nChoose **A4** or **US Letter** when the receiving person or printer expects standard paper. **Automatic** orientation chooses portrait or landscape separately for each image; you can also select either orientation yourself.\\n\\nThe image fits inside the selected margins without cropping or stretching. This changes its printed size and effective PPI to fit the paper. If its proportions differ from the page, unused space remains. Selecting no margins does not guarantee edge-to-edge coverage on a differently shaped page, and a physical printer may impose its own unprintable borders.\\n\\n## Understand transparent backgrounds\\n\\nChoose **Transparent** to retain transparent image areas, or **White** to place them on white. The checkerboard in the converter is only a preview aid; it is not added to the PDF.\\n\\nPDF readers usually display transparent pages on white. A white-looking page in a reader therefore does not, by itself, tell you whether image transparency was flattened. Check the downloaded PDF in the application where you intend to use it.\\n\\n<figure className=\\"png-pdf-figure\\">\\n  <a href=\\"/tutorials/media/png-to-pdf-a4-transparent.png\\" aria-label=\\"Open full-size screenshot of the paper and transparency settings\\">\\n    <img src=\\"/tutorials/media/png-to-pdf-a4-transparent.png\\" width=\\"1088\\" height=\\"506\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" alt=\\"The converter shows A4, Automatic orientation, 10 mm margins and Transparent selected, alongside the landscape parrot preview.\\" />\\n  </a>\\n  <figcaption>Use the preview to check placement and margins. Check the downloaded PDF for fine detail and its appearance in your intended viewer.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## Check the downloaded PDF, not just the preview\\n\\nThe converter\'s small previews show page layout. They are not a full-resolution proof of fine detail or color.\\n\\nAfter selecting **Create PDF** and **Download PDF**, open the saved file and check:\\n\\n1. **Page order and placement.** Confirm that every intended image appears and that the paper, orientation and margins are suitable.\\n2. **Fine detail.** Inspect important text or lines at a useful viewing scale. Raster images can show pixels when magnified far enough, even when their pixel dimensions are preserved.\\n3. **Color and transparency.** Compare the result in the application you will actually use. Supported profiles and bit depth are preserved, but viewer and printer color management can affect appearance. Keep the original PNG for future editing or metadata you may need.\\n4. **Print size.** Check the PDF page dimensions and the printer\'s scaling settings. A viewer\'s \u201cfit to page\u201d print option can change the physical size again.\\n\\n## Common questions about quality\\n\\n### Why is my PDF larger than the original PNGs?\\n\\nA PDF has its own document structure and image representation. Transparency and image content also affect the output size. This tool does not guarantee a smaller file. The output limit is 128 MB; try a smaller batch if you reach it.\\n\\n### Does local processing guarantee identical colors?\\n\\nNo. Processing location and color handling are separate questions. This converter preserves original pixel values, bit depth and supported color profiles while processing locally. The PDF reader, screen profile, printer, paper and print settings can still affect the visible result. Untagged images use an sRGB assumption; check the downloaded PDF in your intended viewing or printing workflow.\\n\\n### Can I make a low-resolution screenshot sharper by converting it?\\n\\nConversion does not recover missing detail. Start with a higher-resolution original when possible. Enlarging a small raster image spreads the existing pixels over a larger area.\\n\\n### Does changing margins resize the embedded image?\\n\\nWith **Fit to image**, margins add space around the image without changing its printed size. With **A4** or **US Letter**, margins change the area available for the image and therefore its printed size. The embedded pixel dimensions stay the same in either case.\\n\\nFor the complete selection, ordering and download workflow, follow [the free PNG to PDF conversion guide](./../2026-11-08-png-to-pdf-free-online/index.md). You can then [open the converter](https://phcode.dev/tools/png-to-pdf.html) and choose settings for your actual sharing or printing task."},{"id":"release-5.5","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/release-5.5","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-10-07-Release-5.5/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-07-Release-5.5/index.md","title":"Phoenix Code 5.5.11: Layers, Visual AI & ChatGPT Codex","description":"See and edit your page\u2019s HTML structure, try design ideas with Phoenix AI, and bring ChatGPT Codex and Claude Code CLI into a visual workflow. Plus simpler AI setup, demos, usage graphs, and photo search.","date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Live Preview Edit","permalink":"/blog/tags/live-preview-edit"},{"inline":true,"label":"AI","permalink":"/blog/tags/ai"},{"inline":true,"label":"Claude Code","permalink":"/blog/tags/claude-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"Git","permalink":"/blog/tags/git"},{"inline":true,"label":"Phoenix Pro","permalink":"/blog/tags/phoenix-pro"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":7.04,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Devansh Agarwal","title":"Founding Engineer, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/devvaannsh","imageURL":"https://github.com/devvaannsh.png","key":"devvaannsh","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Krrish Parmar","title":"Designer, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/Electrofist","imageURL":"https://github.com/Electrofist.png","key":"electrofist","page":null},{"name":"Ansu Ann Koshy","title":"Product Growth, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/AnsuKoshy","imageURL":"https://github.com/AnsuKoshy.png","key":"ansu","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-5.5","title":"Phoenix Code 5.5.11: Layers, Visual AI & ChatGPT Codex","description":"See and edit your page\u2019s HTML structure, try design ideas with Phoenix AI, and bring ChatGPT Codex and Claude Code CLI into a visual workflow. Plus simpler AI setup, demos, usage graphs, and photo search.","authors":["arun","devvaannsh","charly","electrofist","ansu","phoenixBot"],"tags":["Live Preview Edit","AI","Claude Code","Git","Phoenix Pro","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"PNG to PDF Quality: Pixels, Color and Print Size","permalink":"/blog/how-to-convert-png-to-pdf-without-losing-quality"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phoenix Code 5.2: Styles Bar, Code Intelligence & More","permalink":"/blog/release-5.2"}},"content":"Phoenix Code 5.5.11 is now available at [phcode.dev](https://phcode.dev).\\n\\nSee and edit your HTML with the new [Layers panel](#explore-your-page-with-layers), [try AI design ideas on your page](#try-the-options-on-your-page), [explore interactive replies in chat](#interactive-replies-in-chat), and [bring your existing CLI agents into Phoenix Code](#bring-your-existing-cli-agents-into-phoenix-code). There\u2019s also [simpler AI setup](#simpler-ai-setup), [AI demos](#ai-demos), [usage graphs](#ai-usage-graphs), [thinking effort](#choose-how-much-ai-thinks), and [photo search](#find-the-right-photo-with-ai).\\n\\n## [Explore and Edit Your Page with Layers](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/layers-panel)\\n\\n**Edit your web pages visually, without touching code!** The new Layers sidebar lets you explore your page\u2019s HTML structure, add, move, duplicate, or delete elements, and edit their properties.\\n\\nChange colors, fonts, sizes, and spacing with simple visual controls. Every change is instantly reflected in your code and Live Preview.\\n\\n> The Layers panel is a Phoenix Pro feature.\\n\\n![Image](https://docs-images.phcode.dev/in-app/pro-layersPanel.jpg)\\n\\n## [Ask AI in Live Preview](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/ai-ask)\\n\\n**Just point and ask AI, instead of explaining what you want to change!** Select any part of your page in Live Preview and ask AI to make changes. No need to describe where or what you\'re referring to.\\n\\nAsk AI to suggest alternative designs, explore color schemes, or try different fonts.\\n\\n**Ask AI also works with [custom server previews](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/Features/Live%20Preview/live-preview-settings)**, including PHP and other server-rendered pages, as well as selected code or Markdown text.\\n\\n> Available only in desktop apps.\\n\\n![](https://user-cdn.phcode.site/images/50f6bd8b-6d22-436f-ad53-8674891c457e.jpeg)\\n\\n## [Intelligent UI in AI Chat](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/ai-chat)\\n\\n**See what AI means, instead of just reading about it!** Phoenix AI can now show interactive designs, buttons, cards, layouts, and more directly in chat.\\n\\nExplore different styles, click through options, and adjust controls right in the conversation. Instead of just describing an idea, AI can show you how it looks and works\u2014all without opening Live Preview or writing code.\\n\\n> This feature is available only in desktop apps.\\n\\n![](https://user-cdn.phcode.site/images/6d84fa6b-0a0c-47a7-b0b6-499cc20ec5fb.png)\\n\\n## [Bring Your Claude Code & Codex CLI to Phoenix Code](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/ai-cli)\\n\\n**Supercharge your existing CLI agents with rich, interactive visual workflows!** Use your existing Claude Code or Codex subscriptions in Phoenix Code to explore designs in Live Preview, try interactive options, and make changes visually instead of relying on text descriptions.\\n\\nRun multiple CLI sessions, switch between them easily, and send **Ask AI** requests directly to your agents. Track your AI consumption with the built-in **AI Usage Meter** (see below).\\n\\n> Running CLI agents inside Phoenix Code is a Phoenix Pro feature, available only in desktop apps.\\n\\n![](https://user-cdn.phcode.site/images/117a072b-efd1-4623-87d7-5c20746a9b6b.gif)\\n\\n## [Track Your AI Usage](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/ai-chat)\\n\\n**See how much AI you\'re using, all in one place!** Track token usage and estimated costs across Phoenix AI, Claude Code, and Codex CLI sessions.\\n\\nExplore daily usage graphs, monthly trends, and breakdowns by AI model. You can also clear your usage history without deleting your chats.\\n\\n> Costs are estimated using API list prices and may differ from your actual provider charges. Available only in desktop apps.\\n\\n![](https://user-cdn.phcode.site/images/84bdfaf0-31c1-45eb-8a59-3107179f7c04.png)\\n\\n## [Easier AI Setup & Quick Access](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/ai-chat)\\n\\n**Get started with your favorite AI tools more easily!** The new AI start screen brings Phoenix AI, ChatGPT Codex, and Claude Code CLI together in one place.\\n\\nQuickly set up your AI tools, switch running sessions, and pick up right where you left off.\\n\\n![Image](https://docs-images.phcode.dev/in-app/pro-aiPanel.jpg)\\n\\n## [AI Demos](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/ai-chat)\\n\\nSee what you can create with Phoenix AI before signing in or using any AI credits. Watch a page take shape, explore the result, and reveal the prompt behind it. Click **Surprise Me** to try it.\\n\\n> This feature is available only in desktop apps.\\n\\n## [Adjust AI Thinking Effort](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/ai-chat)\\n\\n**Choose how much effort AI puts into solving your problems!** Use lower thinking effort for quick, simple tasks or higher effort for complex problems that need deeper reasoning.\\n\\nSelect your preferred **Thinking Effort** for supported AI models, or leave it at **Default** to use the model\'s standard setting.\\n\\n> This feature is available only in desktop apps.\\n\\n![](https://user-cdn.phcode.site/images/3428ceb1-402d-4410-86ca-552bc02805b9.png)\\n\\n## [Discover Stock Photos with AI](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/ai-images)\\n\\n**Find the perfect photos for your website with the help of AI!** Explore real stock photos, discover new ideas, and let AI help you narrow down your choices to find images that fit your design.\\n\\nAsk AI to add your favorite photos directly to your page or download them into your project.\\n\\n> This feature is available only in desktop apps.\\n\\n![Image](https://docs-images.phcode.dev/in-app/pro-aiImages.jpg)\\n\\n## [Larger Image Previews in Markdown](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/markdown-editor)\\n\\n**Take a closer look at images without leaving your Markdown document!** Open images in a larger view to see details more clearly, whether you\'re reading or editing.\\n\\nSimply click an image in reader mode, or double-click it while editing. Press **Escape** to return to your document.\\n\\n## Terminal and Git improvements\\n\\n- **Name your terminals.** Rename terminal tabs so you can tell your development server, build, and shell apart.\\n- **Keep your bearings after a project switch.** A notice lets you keep existing terminals running or restart them in the new project\'s folder, with confirmation when active processes would be stopped.\\n- **Open terminal links.** Applications that print clickable terminal hyperlinks can now open them in your default browser.\\n- **See what has been pushed.** Git history marks the last pushed commit and identifies the local commits above it. Branches that have not been pushed get a clear indication too.\\n- **Open projects without Git interruptions.** Projects without a repository no longer trigger a \u201cgetting remotes\u201d error dialog.\\n\\n## Notable fixes and performance improvements\\n\\n- Fixed a race where a delayed file reload could overwrite text typed or saved while the reload was in progress. ([Change](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commit/e00a5a588))\\n- Fixed stale Markdown preview updates reverting newer edits made in the code editor. ([Change](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commit/f2f2382fb))\\n- Live Preview reconnects after a dropped connection, and incomplete HTML is handled more reliably while you type. ([Reconnect](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commit/44e914f2c), [HTML editing](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commit/9b57186ae))\\n- Reduced repeated CSS parsing, page measurements, and tree rebuilding during selection and editing in Live Preview and Layers.\\n- Fixed JavaScript hints getting stuck while indexing HTML or PHP, and older project preferences arriving after a project switch. ([Hints](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commit/1198f7f3a), [Preferences](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commit/b24ad37a0))\\n- Python and PHP language-server setup now limits automatic retries and keeps error details and a manual retry available in the task list. Python tooling also updates to Pyrefly 1.2.0 and Ruff 0.16.5. ([Setup](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commit/9325978d4), [Tooling](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commit/391fd391b))\\n- Fixed the sidebar resize handle being left over the editor after the sidebar was collapsed. ([Change](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commit/e0107ea35))\\n- Dropping several files now leaves the last file in the drop order active, regardless of which one loads fastest. ([Change](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commit/9d896d2c7))\\n- Notifications stay open while you hover over them, giving you time to read or use their actions. ([Change](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commit/a40ab64e9))\\n- AI plan review now handles approval, revision, cancellation, and stopping more consistently. Chat also resumes following new messages after you have scrolled back to read older replies.\\n\\n## Platform and account notes\\n\\n- **Windows:** Fixed AI multiline edits being rejected when the file and the AI used different line endings. Language-server quick fixes also handle drive-letter capitalization correctly.\\n- **macOS:** Improved semibold and bold text rendering in the interface.\\n- **Managed desktop installations:** Administrators can point the updater at a custom update manifest, including a custom Linux installer URL.\\n- **Accounts:** Desktop sign-in refreshes Pro access immediately, and existing subscribers no longer receive prompts to buy Pro again.\\n\\nGet the update at [phcode.dev](https://phcode.dev), and let us know what you build in [GitHub Discussions](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/discussions)."},{"id":"release-5.2","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/release-5.2","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-08-05-Release-5.2/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-08-05-Release-5.2/index.md","title":"Phoenix Code 5.2: Styles Bar, Code Intelligence & More","description":"Phoenix Code 5.2 introduces the Styles Bar for visual styling in Live Preview, IDE-grade code intelligence for JavaScript, TypeScript, JSON, Python, and PHP, AI model selection, an improved Git panel, and video and audio preview.","date":"2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Styles Bar","permalink":"/blog/tags/styles-bar"},{"inline":true,"label":"Code Intelligence","permalink":"/blog/tags/code-intelligence"},{"inline":true,"label":"Live Preview Edit","permalink":"/blog/tags/live-preview-edit"},{"inline":true,"label":"AI","permalink":"/blog/tags/ai"},{"inline":true,"label":"Claude Code","permalink":"/blog/tags/claude-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"Git","permalink":"/blog/tags/git"},{"inline":true,"label":"Phoenix Pro","permalink":"/blog/tags/phoenix-pro"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":5.33,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Devansh Agarwal","title":"Founding Engineer, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/devvaannsh","imageURL":"https://github.com/devvaannsh.png","key":"devvaannsh","page":null},{"name":"Krrish Parmar","title":"Designer, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/Electrofist","imageURL":"https://github.com/Electrofist.png","key":"electrofist","page":null},{"name":"Ansu Ann Koshy","title":"Product Growth, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/AnsuKoshy","imageURL":"https://github.com/AnsuKoshy.png","key":"ansu","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-5.2","title":"Phoenix Code 5.2: Styles Bar, Code Intelligence & More","description":"Phoenix Code 5.2 introduces the Styles Bar for visual styling in Live Preview, IDE-grade code intelligence for JavaScript, TypeScript, JSON, Python, and PHP, AI model selection, an improved Git panel, and video and audio preview.","authors":["arun","charly","devvaannsh","electrofist","ansu","phoenixBot"],"tags":["Styles Bar","Code Intelligence","Live Preview Edit","AI","Claude Code","Git","Phoenix Pro","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phoenix Code 5.5.11: Layers, Visual AI & ChatGPT Codex","permalink":"/blog/release-5.5"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phoenix Code 5.1: AI, Design Mode & More","permalink":"/blog/release-5.1"}},"content":"import React from \'react\';\\nimport VideoPlayer from \'@site/src/components/Video/player\';\\n\\nPhoenix Code 5.2 is now available at [phcode.io](https://phcode.io).\\n\\nThis release brings the [Styles Bar](#style-your-page-visually-with-the-styles-bar) for styling your page visually, [Code Intelligence](#code-intelligence-for-javascript-typescript-json-python-and-php) for JavaScript, TypeScript, JSON, Python, and PHP, a [Starter Bar](#start-from-a-blank-page) for blank pages, [Doc Comment Generation](#generate-doc-comments), [AI Model Selection](#choose-your-ai-model), an [Improved Git Panel](#git-improvements), and [Video and Audio Preview](#video-and-audio-preview) right in the editor.\\n\\n## Style your page visually with the Styles Bar\\n\\nSelect an element in Live Preview and the new Styles Bar appears with controls for fonts, colors, backgrounds, borders, spacing, and layout. Every change syncs to your code in real time.\\n\\nPick where your edits are saved - inline on the element, in an existing CSS rule, or in a new rule the bar creates for you. You can also style `hover`, `focus`, and other element states, and the Live Preview turns the state on while you edit.\\n\\nThe font picker covers system fonts and the full Google Fonts collection, with each font previewed in its own style. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/styles-bar)\\n\\n<VideoPlayer\\n  src=\\"https://docs-images.phcode.dev/videos/styles-bar/styles-bar-hero.mp4\\"\\n/>\\n\\n## Code Intelligence for JavaScript, TypeScript, JSON, Python, and PHP\\n\\nPhoenix Code now ships IDE-grade code intelligence: context-aware completions with documentation beside them, parameter hints, hover info, jump to definition, find usages, and live error checking with quick fixes.\\n\\n- **JavaScript & TypeScript** - full project intelligence with auto imports and automatically managed configuration.\\n- **JSON** - schema-based completion and validation for well-known config files. In `package.json`, get npm package name and version suggestions as you type, and dependencies with known vulnerabilities are flagged.\\n- **Python** - type-aware intelligence via Pyrefly, with Ruff powering code formatting.\\n- **PHP** - full intelligence via Intelephense, installed on demand when you open a PHP file.\\n\\nLanguage servers set themselves up automatically in the background and run locally on your machine, so your code is not sent anywhere. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/code-intelligence)\\n\\n<VideoPlayer\\n  src=\\"https://docs-images.phcode.dev/videos/code-intelligence/code-intelligence.mp4\\"\\n/>\\n\\n> Code intelligence powered by language servers is available only in desktop apps.\\n\\n## Choose your AI model\\n\\nPick which Claude model powers your AI chat, right from the chat header, and switch models even in the middle of a conversation.\\n\\nPrefer an editor without AI? It can now be switched off completely from `View > Enable AI` or the AI settings dialog. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/ai-models)\\n\\n![AI model selection](https://docs-images.phcode.dev/in-app/pro-aiModels.png)\\n\\n## Start from a blank page\\n\\nEdit Mode needs an element to work on, and a blank page has none. Now a Starter Bar appears on empty pages so you can add your first element right from the preview, and Phoenix Code creates the page structure for you. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/live-preview-edit)\\n\\n<VideoPlayer\\nsrc=\\"https://docs-images.phcode.dev/videos/live-preview-edit/starter-bar.mp4\\"\\n/>\\n\\n## Generate Doc Comments\\n\\nType `/**` on the line above a function or class and accept the hint that appears. Phoenix Code reads the signature and generates a documentation comment with every parameter filled in, in each language\'s own convention: JSDoc for JavaScript and TypeScript, PHPDoc for PHP, docstrings for Python, and more. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/doc-comments)\\n\\n<VideoPlayer\\n  src=\\"https://docs-images.phcode.dev/videos/doc-comments/jsdoc-generation.mp4\\"\\n/>\\n\\n## Git improvements\\n\\nYour current branch is now shown in the sidebar and on the Git panel toolbar. Click either one to switch branches or create a new one.\\n\\nThe panel\'s menu also gets a new `Open All Changed Files` action that opens every modified file at once. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/git)\\n\\n![Git branch dropdown](./branch-dropdown.png)\\n\\n## Video and Audio Preview\\n\\nClick a video or audio file in your project to play it right inside the editor, with the file\'s dimensions, duration, and size shown alongside. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/video-audio-preview)\\n\\n![Video preview](https://docs-images.phcode.dev/in-app/videoAudioPreview.jpg)\\n\\n## Notable changes and fixes\\n\\n- Pasting from Excel and other spreadsheets now inserts text instead of an image. ([#3033](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/3033))\\n- Editing an element\'s attributes in source code now updates the Control Box immediately. ([#3020](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/3020))\\n- Element selection in Live Preview no longer lands on the wrong element after property edits. ([#3055](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/3055))\\n- Media and image previews now show the file\'s real path instead of an internal path. ([#3032](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/3032))\\n- The Markdown editor\'s slash menu now opens at the cursor instead of the right edge. ([#2940](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2940))\\n- The Markdown editor no longer indents image-only links on save. ([#2937](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2937))\\n- Live Preview toolbar buttons are no longer hidden by long file names. ([#2942](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2942))\\n- Quick access panel buttons no longer overflow when the bottom panel is small. ([#2945](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2945))\\n- Opening the terminal now exits Design Mode correctly. ([#2915](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2915))\\n- Fixed the Git toolbar icon causing exceptions at startup. ([#2904](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2904))\\n- An expired Claude Code login is now detected, with a prompt to log in again from the chat panel. ([#3065](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/3065))\\n- The Claude CLI is now detected when installed via Homebrew or Linuxbrew. ([#2897](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2897))\\n- The guided tour now appears for users who updated from older versions. ([#2892](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2892))\\n\\n## Performance & Stability\\n\\n- Fixed sidebar resize issues and removed unused title bar space in desktop apps. ([#3021](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/3021))\\n- Working set selection bugs fixed and rendering performance improved. ([#3039](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/3039))\\n- Screenshots are sharper on high-DPI displays and no longer fail from rounding errors. ([#2947](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2947), [#2953](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2953))\\n\\n## Platform Notes\\n\\n### Linux\\n\\n- Fixed auto-update failing to detect the installed AppImage path. ([#2926](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2926), [#2927](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2927))"},{"id":"release-5.1","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/release-5.1","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-05-08-Release-5.1/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-08-Release-5.1/index.md","title":"Phoenix Code 5.1: AI, Design Mode & More","description":"Phoenix Code 5.1 introduces an AI assistant powered by Claude Code, a brand new Design Mode, a rich Markdown editor, an integrated terminal, and Phoenix Neo themes.","date":"2026-05-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"AI","permalink":"/blog/tags/ai"},{"inline":true,"label":"Claude Code","permalink":"/blog/tags/claude-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"Design Mode","permalink":"/blog/tags/design-mode"},{"inline":true,"label":"Markdown Editor","permalink":"/blog/tags/markdown-editor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Terminal","permalink":"/blog/tags/terminal"},{"inline":true,"label":"Live Preview Edit","permalink":"/blog/tags/live-preview-edit"},{"inline":true,"label":"Phoenix Pro","permalink":"/blog/tags/phoenix-pro"},{"inline":true,"label":"Phoenix Neo","permalink":"/blog/tags/phoenix-neo"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":5.74,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Devansh Agarwal","title":"Founding Engineer, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/devvaannsh","imageURL":"https://github.com/devvaannsh.png","key":"devvaannsh","page":null},{"name":"Krrish Parmar","title":"Designer, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/Electrofist","imageURL":"https://github.com/Electrofist.png","key":"electrofist","page":null},{"name":"Ansu Ann Koshy","title":"Product Growth, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/AnsuKoshy","imageURL":"https://github.com/AnsuKoshy.png","key":"ansu","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-5.1","title":"Phoenix Code 5.1: AI, Design Mode & More","description":"Phoenix Code 5.1 introduces an AI assistant powered by Claude Code, a brand new Design Mode, a rich Markdown editor, an integrated terminal, and Phoenix Neo themes.","authors":["arun","charly","devvaannsh","electrofist","ansu","phoenixBot"],"tags":["AI","Claude Code","Design Mode","Markdown Editor","Terminal","Live Preview Edit","Phoenix Pro","Phoenix Neo","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phoenix Code 5.2: Styles Bar, Code Intelligence & More","permalink":"/blog/release-5.2"},"nextItem":{"title":"Brackets Editor in 2026: Phoenix Code Picks Up Where It Left Off","permalink":"/blog/brackets-editor-phoenix-code"}},"content":"import React from \'react\';\\nimport VideoPlayer from \'@site/src/components/Video/player\';\\n\\nPhoenix Code 5.1 is now available at [phcode.io](https://phcode.io). \\n\\nThis release brings AI into the editor with [Claude Code](#ai-with-visual-and-runtime-context), a new [Design Mode](#design-mode) for visual editing, a rich [Markdown editor](#rich-markdown-editor), a built-in [terminal](#built-in-terminal), responsive [device previews](#view-your-page-at-any-device-size), a [rebuilt Linux app](#linux), and a fresh new look with the [Phoenix Neo](#phoenix-neo-themes) themes.\\n\\n## AI with visual and runtime context\\n\\nPhoenix Code now includes an AI assistant powered by Claude Code, designed to support your creative workflow - not replace it.\\n\\nMost AI coding agents only read source code. AI in Phoenix Code looks at the rendered page too. It sees what the browser shows you, not just what the stylesheet or code implies.\\n\\nAsk why a layout feels off, fix tricky CSS issues, improve spacing or typography, or attach files and screenshots to show exactly what you mean. Bring AI in when you need it, and keep creating without leaving your flow.\\n\\n<VideoPlayer\\n  src=\\"https://docs-images.phcode.dev/website/videos/ai-pro-dialog.mp4\\"\\n/>\\n\\n## Design Mode\\n\\nFocus fully on your content while your code stays out of the way. Work side by side with AI and Live Preview. Edit content visually, whether you\'re building web pages or Markdown documents. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/design-mode)\\n\\n![Design Mode](https://docs-images.phcode.dev/in-app/pro-designMode.png)\\n\\n## Rich Markdown Editor\\n\\nEdit Markdown like a document in Live Preview. Format text, edit tables, and it\u2019s easy to add images - just paste or pick one from your device, like in Google Docs or Word.\\n\\nAn easier way to create Markdown files that you can share with others. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/markdown-editor)\\n\\n<VideoPlayer\\n  src=\\"https://docs-images.phcode.dev/website/videos/markdown-pro-dialog.mp4\\"\\n/>\\n\\n## Live Preview Edit Upgrades\\n\\nLive Preview Edit just got a major upgrade. You can format text with bold, italics, and more. Add new elements and update tags, classes, and attributes, all directly in Live Preview. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/live-preview-edit)\\n\\n![Live Preview Edit](https://docs-images.phcode.dev/in-app/pro-controlBox.png)\\n\\n## View your page at any device size\\n\\nNow you can quickly see how your page looks at different device sizes right inside Phoenix Code with a single click.\\n\\nPhoenix Code also picks up your CSS `@media` breakpoints so you can jump straight to where your layout switches. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/resize-ruler)\\n\\n<VideoPlayer\\n  src=\\"https://docs-images.phcode.dev/website/videos/device-size-pro-dialog.mp4\\"\\n/>\\n\\n## Built-in Terminal\\n\\nPhoenix Code now has a built-in tabbed terminal, so you can run commands right next to your code without switching windows. It supports your preferred shell and works across all supported platforms. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/terminal)\\n\\n![Built-in Terminal](https://docs-images.phcode.dev/in-app/terminal.png)\\n\\n## New Linux Platform\\n\\nPhoenix Code for Linux has been rebuilt from the ground up.\\n\\nThe previous Linux app was harder to install and didn\u2019t match the experience we wanted to deliver. We heard that feedback loud and clear. This release is powered by a brand-new Linux platform, bringing faster performance, easier installation, and a desktop experience that now stands alongside Windows and macOS.\\n\\n\\n## All Your Tools, One Place\\n\\nWe\'ve changed how the Bottom Panel works to make it easier to access all your tools with a new tools drawer interface. Git, Terminal, Problems, and more are now all in one place. You can switch between them instantly or open multiple tools at once. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/git)\\n\\n![Bottom Panel](https://docs-images.phcode.dev/in-app/bottom-panel.png)\\n\\n## Phoenix Neo Themes\\n\\nMeet `Phoenix Dark Neo` and `Phoenix Light Neo`, the redesigned default themes with a cleaner, modernized look.\\n\\nSwitch between them anytime from `View > Themes...`. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/app-links/themes)\\n\\n![Themes](https://docs-images.phcode.dev/in-app/themes-settings.png)\\n\\n## Notable changes and fixes\\n\\n- Prevented an out-of-memory crash that could occur while indexing very large projects \u2014 Phoenix now detects binary files and caps its index cache so big repos stay smooth. ([#2801](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2801))\\n- Phoenix now recovers gracefully from a corrupted local database instead of crashing on startup. ([#2782](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2782))\\n- Fixed a reflected XSS issue on the \\"browser not supported\\" page. ([#2851](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2851))\\n- Fixed `File > Quit` not closing multi-window sessions on the desktop app. ([#2639](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2639))\\n- Fixed a thin white line at the edges of Live Preview. ([#2855](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2855))\\n- Fixed a modal overlay that could remain on screen when dialogs opened in quick succession. ([#2789](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2789))\\n- Fixed Find in Files button placement when the input boxes were empty. ([#2680](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2680))\\n- Fixed an issue where some pro trial users were being signed out early. ([#2658](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2658))\\n\\n## Performance & Stability\\n\\n- Sidebar layout is now preserved across hide and show, making expansion feel near-instant. ([#2834](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2834))\\n- Fixed a race condition during Live Preview load that could cause a stale page to overwrite a fresh one. ([#2668](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2668))\\n- Tightened Live Preview document handling and reduced editor event leaks. ([#2671](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2671))\\n- Improved Live Preview edit performance for larger pages. ([#2793](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2793))\\n- Smoother scrollbar behavior in the sidebar. ([#2726](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2726), [#2727](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2727))\\n\\n## Platform Notes\\n\\n### Linux\\n\\n- The desktop app is now built on a **Chromium-based renderer**, replacing the previous WebKit2GTK build (which was painfully slow). It ships as a **single AppImage** that runs across most major distributions, and now supports **auto-update**, bringing it to parity with macOS and Windows. ([#2635](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2635), [#2637](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2637), [#2651](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2651))\\n\\n### macOS\\n\\n- Avoided the Xcode \\"git install\\" dialog popping up at startup on Macs without git. ([#2662](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2662))\\n- Fixed new-window dimensions being slightly off due to inner/outer measurement mix-up. ([#2720](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2720))\\n\\n### Windows\\n\\n- Fixed window size jumping when opening a new window from `File > New Window`. ([#2723](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2723))\\n\\n## All changes\\n\\nPlease see [this link](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commits/main/?since=2026-01-18&until=2026-05-22) for a full list of changes in GitHub.\\n\\n## A Request from the Phoenix Team:\\n\\n- **Share your feedback:** https://github.com/orgs/phcode-dev/discussions\\n- **Spread the word** about Phoenix to friends and colleagues.\\n\\nWith gratitude,\\n\\nThe Phoenix Team"},{"id":"brackets-editor-phoenix-code","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/brackets-editor-phoenix-code","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-03-04-phoenix-code-vs-brackets/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-04-phoenix-code-vs-brackets/index.md","title":"Brackets Editor in 2026: Phoenix Code Picks Up Where It Left Off","description":"The Brackets editor is in maintenance mode \u2014 Phoenix Code continues where it left off. Same team, same live preview DNA, rebuilt for 2026. Free and open source.","date":"2026-03-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Brackets","permalink":"/blog/tags/brackets"},{"inline":true,"label":"Comparison","permalink":"/blog/tags/comparison"},{"inline":true,"label":"Migration","permalink":"/blog/tags/migration"},{"inline":true,"label":"Code Editor","permalink":"/blog/tags/code-editor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Live Preview","permalink":"/blog/tags/live-preview"},{"inline":true,"label":"Open Source","permalink":"/blog/tags/open-source"}],"readingTime":6.2,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"brackets-editor-phoenix-code","title":"Brackets Editor in 2026: Phoenix Code Picks Up Where It Left Off","description":"The Brackets editor is in maintenance mode \u2014 Phoenix Code continues where it left off. Same team, same live preview DNA, rebuilt for 2026. Free and open source.","authors":["charly"],"tags":["Brackets","Comparison","Migration","Code Editor","Live Preview","Open Source"],"keywords":["brackets editor","adobe brackets","brackets code editor","brackets extensions","phoenix code","live preview editor"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phoenix Code 5.1: AI, Design Mode & More","permalink":"/blog/release-5.1"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phoenix Pro Release (January-2026, V-5.0) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-5.0"}},"content":"If you\'ve used Brackets, Phoenix Code will feel immediately familiar.\\n\\nBuilt by the same team behind Brackets, Phoenix Code keeps Live Preview and fast visual front-end development at its core \u2014 now rebuilt on a modern foundation.\\n\\nPhoenix Code isn\u2019t a tribute or a spiritual successor. It\u2019s the natural continuation of the ideas that started with Brackets.\\n\\nHere\u2019s what\u2019s changed \u2014 and how to switch.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Brackets vs Phoenix Code: What\'s New\\n\\nPhoenix Code includes everything Brackets offered \u2014 Live Preview, lightweight workflow, web-first focus \u2014 plus built-in Git, a browser edition, visual CSS editing, and an active extension marketplace. Here\'s the full comparison.\\n\\n| Feature | Brackets | Phoenix Code |\\n|---------|----------|-------------|\\n| Live Preview | Basic (view only) | Full live preview (editing in preview with [Pro](/docs/Pro%20Features/live-preview-edit)) |\\n| Visual Editing | Limited | Color pickers, number dials, gradient editors, drag-and-drop |\\n| Git Integration | Required third-party extension | Built-in |\\n| Browser Version | No | Yes \u2014 [phcode.dev](https://phcode.dev), no install needed |\\n| Chromebook / Tablet Support | No | Yes |\\n| Extension Marketplace | No longer maintained | Active and growing |\\n| Active Development | In maintenance mode since 2021 | Regular releases, active team |\\n| Open Source | Yes | Yes (AGPL-3.0) |\\n| Built-in Image Library | No | Yes \u2014 stock photos you can drag into projects |\\n| Price | Free | Free ([Pro](https://phcode.dev/pricing) from $9/mo for Live Preview Edit) |\\n\\nThe free version of Phoenix Code covers everything Brackets did and more \u2014 Git, the browser edition, visual editing tools, all included. [Phoenix Pro](/docs/Pro%20Features/live-preview-edit) adds Live Preview Edit, and exists to help sustain full-time development by a small indie team.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## What Happened to the Brackets Editor?\\n\\nBrackets was created at Adobe and built on CEF (Chromium Embedded Framework) \u2014 a technology choice that made sense in 2014 but became increasingly difficult to maintain. Security patches, OS compatibility, performance work \u2014 it all got harder every year. In 2021, we took over Brackets from Adobe, continuing its development under the community we founded and now lead.\\n\\nRather than keep patching an aging foundation, we modernized the platform so it can run anywhere \u2014 in browsers, on desktop with Electron or Tauri, pretty much any modern system. One codebase that works everywhere, from [phcode.dev](https://phcode.dev) in your browser to a native desktop app.\\n\\nThe architecture changed. The team didn\'t. The design philosophy didn\'t. We named it Phoenix Code \u2014 Brackets, reborn.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## What Carried Over from Brackets\\n\\nIf you\'re wondering whether Phoenix Code will feel familiar \u2014 it will.\\n\\n**Live Preview** is still the core of the experience. Edit HTML or CSS and watch the browser update in real time, no manual refresh. This is the feature that defined Brackets, and it\'s still front and center.\\n\\n**The lightweight workflow** is intact. Open a folder, start editing. Everything just works out of the box.\\n\\n**Web-first focus.** HTML, CSS, JavaScript \u2014 that\'s the sweet spot. Phoenix Code is purpose-built for front-end work, not trying to be a general-purpose IDE.\\n\\n**Keyboard shortcuts and UI layout** are familiar. If you had muscle memory in Brackets, most of it still applies.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## What\'s New in Phoenix Code\\n\\nThese are the features the Brackets community asked for \u2014 and we finally built them.\\n\\n### Edit Directly in the Live Preview (Pro)\\n\\nBrackets\' live preview was view-only. You could see changes reflected in real time, but you always had to make edits in the code. With [Phoenix Pro](/docs/Pro%20Features/live-preview-edit), you can click on any element in the live preview and edit it right there \u2014 change text, swap images by dragging, rearrange elements visually. The source code updates automatically.\\n\\n![Phoenix Code live preview modes for editing HTML and CSS visually](../../docs/images/livePreview/lp-mode.png)\\n\\n### Visual CSS Editing\\n\\nBrackets had inline color pickers \u2014 Phoenix Code keeps those and adds number dials you can scrub to adjust CSS values like margins, padding, font sizes, and more. Hover over a number, drag to adjust, and see the result update in live preview instantly.\\n\\n![Phoenix Code CSS number dial for adjusting margins and padding](../../docs/images/number-dial.gif)\\n\\n### Built-in Git\\n\\nPhoenix Code ships with native Git support based on the familiar Brackets Git extension, addressing many of its earlier limitations with a simpler UX and improved reliability. Commit, push, pull, diff, and branch management, all built in.\\n\\n![Built-in Git integration in Phoenix Code \u2014 commit and push](../../docs/images/git-images/git-commit.png)\\n\\n### Runs in Your Browser\\n\\nOpen [phcode.dev](https://phcode.dev) and start editing \u2014 no install or admin privileges needed. Works on Chromebooks, tablets, shared computers, anywhere you have a browser. The web app runs the same core as the desktop app, so for website editing and live preview it\'s just as capable. For Git, AI features, and the full experience, grab the [native app](https://phcode.dev/download).\\n\\n### Measurement and Inspection Tools\\n\\nInspect spacing between elements, measure distances, and check alignment directly in the live preview. If you work from design mockups, this replaces the constant back-and-forth between your editor and a separate design tool.\\n\\n![Phoenix Code measurement and inspection tools in live preview](../../docs/images/pro/measurements.png)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## How to Switch from Brackets\\n\\nShort version: open your project folder in Phoenix Code. That\'s it.\\n\\n**No migration needed.** Your project files work as-is. No config conversion, no import wizard. Just open the folder.\\n\\n**Extensions.** The most popular Brackets extensions are now built into Phoenix Code: Emmet for abbreviations, Git for version control, [Beautify/Prettier](/docs/Features/beautify-code) for code formatting, and a Tab Bar for managing open files. The extension marketplace is active and growing for anything else you need.\\n\\n**Learning curve.** Minimal. The UI layout is familiar, the shortcuts are similar, and all the new features are additive \u2014 nothing you relied on was removed. You\'ll be productive in minutes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Frequently Asked Questions\\n\\n### What happened to the Brackets code editor?\\n\\nBrackets was created at Adobe and actively developed until 2021. The Phoenix Code project took over the development of Brackets, and rather than keep patching an aging foundation, we rebuilt it to run in browsers and as a desktop app. Brackets, reborn.\\n\\n### Is Phoenix Code the same as Brackets?\\n\\nPhoenix Code is built by the same team and carries forward the same design philosophy \u2014 Live Preview, lightweight workflow, and a focus on HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. The codebase was rewritten on modern web technologies, but the experience is familiar. If you used Brackets, you\'ll feel at home.\\n\\n### Is Phoenix Code free?\\n\\nYes. The free version includes everything Brackets had and more \u2014 Git, the browser edition, visual editing tools, and the extension marketplace. [Phoenix Pro](/docs/Pro%20Features/live-preview-edit) adds Live Preview Edit starting at $9/month and helps sustain full-time development.\\n\\n### Is Brackets still safe to use?\\n\\nBrackets runs on an outdated version of Chromium that no longer receives security patches. For active web development, Phoenix Code is the maintained alternative with regular updates and security fixes.\\n\\n### Does Phoenix Code work on Chromebook?\\n\\nYes. Open [phcode.dev](https://phcode.dev) in any browser \u2014 no install or admin privileges needed. It works on Chromebooks, tablets, and shared computers.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Try Phoenix Code\\n\\nPhoenix Code was built for the Brackets community by the team behind Brackets.\\nIf you\'ve been waiting for the update Brackets deserved, this is it.\\n\\n- **[Open Phoenix Code in your browser](https://phcode.dev)** \u2014 no install, start immediately\\n- [Download the desktop app](https://phcode.dev/download)\\n- [Live Preview documentation](/docs/Features/Live%20Preview/)\\n- [Edit Mode (Pro)](/docs/Pro%20Features/live-preview-edit)\\n- [Read more about the Brackets legacy](/blog/Blog-Legacy)"},{"id":"release-5.0","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/release-5.0","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2026-01-18-Release-5.0/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-18-Release-5.0/index.md","title":"Phoenix Pro Release (January-2026, V-5.0) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 5.0 introduces Phoenix Pro with Live Preview Edit, Emmet support, Tab Bar, and Custom Snippets. The biggest update yet.","date":"2026-01-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Phoenix Pro","permalink":"/blog/tags/phoenix-pro"},{"inline":true,"label":"Live Preview Edit","permalink":"/blog/tags/live-preview-edit"},{"inline":true,"label":"Emmet","permalink":"/blog/tags/emmet"},{"inline":true,"label":"Tab Bar","permalink":"/blog/tags/tab-bar"},{"inline":true,"label":"Custom Snippets","permalink":"/blog/tags/custom-snippets"},{"inline":true,"label":"Collapse Folders","permalink":"/blog/tags/collapse-folders"},{"inline":true,"label":"Smarter hint suggestions","permalink":"/blog/tags/smarter-hint-suggestions"},{"inline":true,"label":"Stability Improvements","permalink":"/blog/tags/stability-improvements"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":3.73,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Devansh Agarwal","title":"Founding Engineer, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/devvaannsh","imageURL":"https://github.com/devvaannsh.png","key":"devvaannsh","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-5.0","title":"Phoenix Pro Release (January-2026, V-5.0) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 5.0 introduces Phoenix Pro with Live Preview Edit, Emmet support, Tab Bar, and Custom Snippets. The biggest update yet.","authors":["arun","charly","devvaannsh","phoenixBot"],"tags":["Phoenix Pro","Live Preview Edit","Emmet","Tab Bar","Custom Snippets","Collapse Folders","Smarter hint suggestions","Stability Improvements","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Brackets Editor in 2026: Phoenix Code Picks Up Where It Left Off","permalink":"/blog/brackets-editor-phoenix-code"},"nextItem":{"title":"Git It Release (January-2025, V-4.0) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-4.0"}},"content":"import React from \'react\';\\nimport VideoPlayer from \'@site/src/components/Video/player\';\\n\\n2026 January release (5.0) of Phoenix Code is now available for download at [phcode.io](https://phcode.io).\\n\\nOur biggest update yet - introducing `Phoenix Pro` and major upgrades like `Live Preview Edit`, `Emmet`,\\n`Tab Bar`, and `Custom Snippets`.\\n\\n## Phoenix Pro - A Sustainable Future\\n\\nPhoenix Code has been free and open source from day one - and **everything you\u2019ve used so far will remain free forever**, including **Live Preview**.\\n\\nWe\u2019re a small, full-time indie team with **no VC backing**, building Phoenix Code because we believe the web deserves a\\ncode editor that\u2019s genuinely **simple and joyful to use**. We tried to sustain development through community donations\\n([Open Collective](https://opencollective.com/phoenix-ide)), but over the last few years it hasn\u2019t been enough to\\nsupport full-time work - and we reached a hard choice: slow down drastically, or find a sustainable path that keeps Phoenix Code healthy.\\n\\nSo we\u2019re introducing **Phoenix Pro** as an *optional* way to support Phoenix Code\u2019s future - with **one Pro feature: Live Preview Edit**.\\nIf Phoenix Code has helped you, Phoenix Pro is a way to help keep the project moving forward.\\n\\nStudents & educators get **Phoenix Pro for Education** at no cost, because we want classrooms to have easy \\naccess to modern, easy-to-use development tooling. [Read More](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/phoenix-pro-school)\\n\\n## Live Preview Edit\\n\\n*Included with Phoenix Pro.*\\n\\n**Live Preview Edit** lets you make changes directly on your page \u2014 and updates your source code instantly.\\n\\nEdit text, links, and images. Drag & drop to rearrange elements. Cut, copy, paste, and use measurement tools\\nto place things precisely.\\n\\nLearn more: [Live Preview Edit](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/Pro%20Features/live-preview-edit), [Image Gallery](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/Pro%20Features/image-gallery), [Measurements](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/Pro%20Features/measurements).\\n\\n<VideoPlayer\\n  src=\\"https://docs-images.phcode.dev/videos/live-preview-edit/live-preview-edit.mp4\\"\\n/>\\n\\n## Emmet\\n\\n**Emmet** one of our most requested features is finally here.\\n\\nWrite Emmet abbreviations and Phoenix Code shows you hints. Select a hint to expand it into a full code snippet. Code faster than ever. [Read More](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/Features/emmet)\\n\\n<VideoPlayer\\n  src=\\"https://docs-images.phcode.dev/videos/editing-text/emmet-html.mp4\\"\\n/>\\n\\n## Tab Bar\\n\\nTab Bar is here. View all your open files at the top of the editor and switch between them instantly.\\n\\nChoose what works for you -Tab Bar, Working Files, or both at the same time. [Read More](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/file-management#tab-bar)\\n\\n![Tab Bar](../../docs/images/fileManagement/tab-bar.png \\"Tab Bar\\")\\n\\n## Custom Snippets\\n\\nDefine your own code hints with Custom Snippets.\\n\\nCreate hints that expand into full code blocks. You can also add cursor positions so Phoenix Code places your cursor exactly where you need it after expansion. [Read More](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/Features/custom-snippets)\\n\\n<VideoPlayer\\n  src=\\"https://docs-images.phcode.dev/videos/custom-snippets/custom-snippets-main.mp4\\"\\n/>\\n\\n## Collapse Folders\\n\\nAfter working for some time, your project structure gets messy with lots of open and nested directories.\\n\\nBut no worries now. Phoenix Code supports Collapse All Folders, which lets you reset your view by collapsing all expanded folders to their root level in one click. [Read More](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/file-management#collapse-all-folders)\\n\\n<VideoPlayer\\n  src=\\"https://docs-images.phcode.dev/videos/file-management/collapse-folders.mp4\\"\\n/>\\n\\n## Notable changes and fixes\\n\\n- Improved Live Preview to support internal stylesheets and SVGs better than before.\\n- Smarter color hints. Phoenix Code prioritizes your previously used colors. [Read More](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/editing-colors#color-hints)\\n- Git markers now appear in the scrollbar, making it easier to locate changes in a file.\\n- Improved Git so that it doesn\'t show stale project status.\\n- Reduced the number of popups shown when first installing Phoenix.\\n- Fixed an issue where macOS/iOS and browser autocorrect or smart keyboards altered filenames during renames.\\n- Added a dialog to notify users when they have security compromised extensions installed.\\n- Slowed down the Quick View popup on hover as it was interfering with user workflows.\\n- Fixed ghost image appearing in Safari when dragging from CodeMirror.\\n- Long names in Working Files are now truncated for better readability.\\n- Added Horizontal Scroll support. Use Shift + mouse scroll.\\n\\n## All changes\\n\\nPlease see [this link](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commits/main/?since=2025-01-30&until=2026-01-18) for a full list of changes\\nin GitHub.\\n\\n## A Request from the Phoenix Team:\\n\\n-   **Share your feedback:** https://github.com/orgs/phcode-dev/discussions\\n-   **Spread the word** about Phoenix to friends and colleagues.\\n\\nWith gratitude,\\n\\nThe Phoenix Team"},{"id":"release-4.0","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/release-4.0","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2025-01-30-Release-4.0/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-01-30-Release-4.0/index.md","title":"Git It Release (January-2025, V-4.0) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 4.0 introduces built-in Git integration, color previews, and open-in-terminal support. The largest release in the Brackets legacy.","date":"2025-01-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Git","permalink":"/blog/tags/git"},{"inline":true,"label":"Color previews","permalink":"/blog/tags/color-previews"},{"inline":true,"label":"Version Control","permalink":"/blog/tags/version-control"},{"inline":true,"label":"open in terminal","permalink":"/blog/tags/open-in-terminal"},{"inline":true,"label":"Stability Improvements","permalink":"/blog/tags/stability-improvements"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":3.18,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Devansh Agarwal","title":"Founding Engineer, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/devvaannsh","imageURL":"https://github.com/devvaannsh.png","key":"devvaannsh","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-4.0","title":"Git It Release (January-2025, V-4.0) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 4.0 introduces built-in Git integration, color previews, and open-in-terminal support. The largest release in the Brackets legacy.","authors":["arun","charly","devvaannsh","phoenixBot"],"tags":["Git","Color previews","Version Control","open in terminal","Stability Improvements","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phoenix Pro Release (January-2026, V-5.0) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-5.0"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phoenix Code: The Legacy of Brackets - Continued","permalink":"/blog/Blog-Legacy"}},"content":"2025 January release(4.0) of Phoenix Code is now available for download at [phcode.io](https://phcode.io).\\n\\nThis is the largest release we had in a while, and we are happy to announce the next phase of Phoenix Code with\\na major version bump to 4.0.\\n\\n## The legacy of Brackets - Continued\\nA decade-long journey of pushing web development forward: The Brackets story continues with Phoenix Code.\\n[Read more about the story of Brackets and the journey to build Phoenix Code and the Brackets community.](https://docs.phcode.dev/blog/Blog-Legacy)\\n\\nThis release\'s core theme is `Git, Color previews, UX imporovements and stability`.\\n\\n## Git for Desktop\\n\\nGit is finally here.  Integrated Git source control with a clean, intuitive interface.\\nStage changes, commit, and sync with a single click while keeping your focus on the code.\\nNow available in the Windows, Mac and Linux desktop apps of Phoenix code. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/Features/git)\\n\\nOur Git integration is built upon the incredible work of the developers behind [Brackets-Git](https://github.com/brackets-userland/brackets-git),\\nwhose contributions made this possible. Thank you!\\n\\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/aeacc7c0-3ee3-4a80-9b05-f2b48140bcdd)\\n\\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6e7b9faf-d57b-448b-a5bb-a111c67489b5)\\n\\n## Color Previews\\n\\nPreview the color(s) used in the file in the gutter area. Hovering over a color box highlights the corresponding color\\ntext in the editor to quickly jump to editing that color. [Read More...](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/editing-colors#color-preview)\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/bedecc32-761b-448e-aced-61828ad3fec6)\\n\\n## Open in Terminal and File Explorer\\n\\nNew \\"Open In\\" feature lets you instantly open directories and files in the system Terminal or File Explorer across\\nWindows, macOS, and Linux desktop apps!\\n\\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/710330ae-8b34-4a51-a1f9-4c02736ed9bd)\\n\\n## Better Translations\\n\\nWe\'ve moved to an LLM-assisted translation framework, delivering higher-quality translations for non-English locales.\\n\\n## Notable changes and fixes\\n\\n- We\'ve removed some inconsistent and rarely used keyboard shortcuts to free up more key combinations for customization.\\n  [Easily configure your own keyboard shortcuts through the UI.](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/Features/keyboard-shortcuts)\\n- Fixed `Option-[shift]-left` and `option-[shift]-right` keyboard shortcuts not working in mac to select/move words.\\n- Survey popups are now less intrusive, appearing as notifications without disrupting your workflow.\\n- Moved back `line:ch` status to left of statusbar. Go to line will accept `:ln:col` format along with `:ln,col`. ![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7429ae42-cb56-444c-8546-6d565bedaaf9)\\n- [Remove specific files from Recent Files](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2035)\\n- Fixes [holding ctrl key in windows should not ui overlay mode.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2015)\\n- [fix: `cmd-click` in mac app and safari didn\'t open context menu.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1994)\\n- [Switching between Ctrl+F and Ctrl+Shift+F now correctly updates the search box with the latest selection.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1999)\\n- [fix: clicking on prevent defaulted A tags in desktop live preview opens browser tab.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1997)\\n \\n## Performance & Stability Tweaks\\n\\n-   [Faster boot times with extension compression and load changes](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2055)\\n    consistency and performance, especially on Safari and macOS.\\n-   [File recovery should only be shown on crash, and fix some file recovery issues.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/2082)\\n\\n## All changes\\n\\nPlease see [this link](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commits/main/?since=2024-11-27&until=2025-01-30) for a full list of changes\\nin GitHub.\\n\\n## A Request from the Phoenix Team:\\n\\n-   **Share your feedback:** https://github.com/orgs/phcode-dev/discussions\\n-   **Spread the word** about Phoenix to friends and colleagues.\\n-   [**Consider supporting us** on GitHub Sponsors](https://github.com/sponsors/phcode-dev). Every contribution helps us\\n    to keep improving Phoenix Code.\\n\\nWith gratitude,\\n\\nThe Phoenix Team"},{"id":"Blog-Legacy","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/Blog-Legacy","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2025-01-30-The Legacy of Brackets - Continued/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-01-30-The Legacy of Brackets - Continued/index.md","title":"Phoenix Code: The Legacy of Brackets - Continued","description":"The story of Brackets \u2014 from its birth at Adobe to Phoenix Code. How a community kept the spirit of Brackets alive and built its future.","date":"2025-01-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Brackets","permalink":"/blog/tags/brackets"},{"inline":true,"label":"Continued","permalink":"/blog/tags/continued"},{"inline":true,"label":"Phoenix Code","permalink":"/blog/tags/phoenix-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"History","permalink":"/blog/tags/history"}],"readingTime":2.55,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"Blog-Legacy","title":"Phoenix Code: The Legacy of Brackets - Continued","description":"The story of Brackets \u2014 from its birth at Adobe to Phoenix Code. How a community kept the spirit of Brackets alive and built its future.","authors":["arun","charly"],"tags":["Brackets","Continued","Phoenix Code","History"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Git It Release (January-2025, V-4.0) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-4.0"},"nextItem":{"title":"Editor Experience Release (November-2024, V-3.10) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.10"}},"content":"Some stories refuse to end because of the people who believe in them. Brackets was one such story. Born at Adobe,\\nit wasn\u2019t just a code editor; it was a vision to bridge the gap between designers and developers on\\nhow we build for the web. But when its creators moved on, the future of Brackets seemed uncertain.\\n\\nYet, Brackets lived on. Too many people loved it, relied on it, and believed in its potential. From that\\npassion, Phoenix Code was born\u2014not as a replacement, but as a continuation of everything Brackets stood for.\\nJoin us as we trace this journey from the birth of Brackets to the rise of Phoenix Code\u2014and discover how a\\ncommunity\u2019s dedication kept the spirit of Brackets alive.\\n\\n## Brackets 1.0 - The Beginning\\nBrackets 1.0 was released 10 years ago at Adobe, on the 4th of November 2014. At the time, Atom from GitHub was\\nthe only peer editor built on the same web-based architecture as Brackets. The web was a very different place then.\\nAdobe was preparing for a post-Flash world (Read: Thoughts on Flash - an open letter by Steve Jobs, April 2010).\\nJust a month earlier, in October 2014, HTML5 had become a W3C Recommendation.\\n\\nBrackets was born as an editor built on web standards, designed for building the web\u2014a window into the future\\nfrom Adobe. Adobe was mostly right in this vision. Visual Studio Code (VSCode), built on similar web technologies,\\nhas since risen to dominate the landscape of code editors.\\n\\nBrackets was created to serve as a bridge between Designer and Developer workflows before the time of Figma and XD.\\nHowever, Adobe was primarily about design tools. As a free developer tool, Brackets was never able to find a place\\namong its design-focused peers.\\n\\n## Brackets 2.0 - The Brackets Open Source Community is Born\\n\\nIn January 2022, Adobe [transitioned the development of Brackets](https://x.com/brackets/status/1480581149604782080) to the newly formed Brackets Community.\\nVersion 2.0 focused on establishing an independent foothold to continue Brackets\' development.\\n\\nWe kick-started the Phoenix Code project as we recognized the need to address nearly three years of\\ndevelopment backlog. This laid the groundwork for the next generation of Brackets.\\n\\n## Phoenix Code - 3.0, The Next Generation of Brackets\\n\\nWith Phoenix Code, we built the foundation to run Brackets almost anywhere with a web browser. The first web\\nversion launched in June 2023, followed by desktop builds in February 2024.\\n\\nVersion 3.0 was primarily about achieving feature parity and stability with Brackets\u2014and then surpassing it.\\n\\n## Phoenix Code - 4.0, Present & Future\\n\\nWe start this year(2025) with the [release of Phoenix Code 4.0](https://docs.phcode.dev/blog/release-4.0)- as we reach feature parity with Brackets and exceeds it\\nin most cases. With this milestone, we return to the original mission of Brackets - to serve as a\\nbridge between Designer and Developer workflows for the Web. To help *people* get things done simpler and faster.\\n\\nWith immense gratitude for our users\' support, we\'re excited to begin this next chapter of Phoenix Code."},{"id":"release-3.10","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/release-3.10","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2024-11-24-Release-3.10/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2024-11-24-Release-3.10/index.md","title":"Editor Experience Release (November-2024, V-3.10) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.10 improves the editor UI with indent guide lines, better performance, and visual feedback. Updated extension developer docs.","date":"2024-11-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Editor Experience","permalink":"/blog/tags/editor-experience"},{"inline":true,"label":"UI Improvements","permalink":"/blog/tags/ui-improvements"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":2.09,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Devansh Agarwal","title":"Founding Engineer, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/devvaannsh","imageURL":"https://github.com/devvaannsh.png","key":"devvaannsh","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-3.10","title":"Editor Experience Release (November-2024, V-3.10) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.10 improves the editor UI with indent guide lines, better performance, and visual feedback. Updated extension developer docs.","authors":["arun","charly","devvaannsh","phoenixBot"],"tags":["Editor Experience","UI Improvements","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phoenix Code: The Legacy of Brackets - Continued","permalink":"/blog/Blog-Legacy"},"nextItem":{"title":"HTML Workflows and stability Release (September-2024, V-3.9) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.9"}},"content":"November release(3.10) of Phoenix Code is now available for download at [phcode.io](https://phcode.io).\\n\\nThis month\'s core theme is `Editor UI improvements`. We have fine-tuned the editing experience for better \\nperformance and visual feedback.\\nWe\'ve also made substantial improvements to [docs.phcode.dev](https://docs.phcode.dev) to better support extension\\ndevelopers.\\n\\n## Indent Guide Lines\\n\\nPhoenix Code now supports indent guidelines to help you better visualize code structure. This feature can\\nbe toggled via `View > Indent Guide Lines`. [Read more...](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/editing-text#indent-guide-lines)\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3d9d0c55-3c9e-4ab3-bbc0-53b563d19e39)\\n\\n## Customizable Editor Line Height\\n\\nTheme Settings (`View > Themes...`) now has a new `Line Height` slider. Adjust the editor line height between 1x and 3x to find your\\nperfect balance of readability and screen real estate. [Read more...](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/editing-text#line-height)\\n\\nAlso added a `Get More...` button to the Themes dialog, linking directly to the themes section in Extension Manager.\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/97a46d9a-e971-401d-8a77-015afa6d5972)\\n\\n## `Line:Col` Indicator Relocation\\n\\nMoved to the right of the status bar, accommodating future status bar extensions. Click on the `line:col` section in the\\nstatusbar to jump to specific lines.\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/540ea992-dda0-4a2c-8042-3c6228616636)\\n\\n## Performance Tweaks\\n\\n-   [Major improvements to editor rendering](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1943) with better editor scroll\\n    consistency and performance, especially on Safari and macOS.\\n-   [Faster startup of extension manager.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1937)\\n\\n## Stability Improvements\\n\\n-   [File recovery now does integrity checks for more reliable crash recovery.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1889)\\n-   [Fixed Markdown image link handling within href tags.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1838)\\n-   [Improved extension registry management to prevent quota issues on Mac/Linux.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1932)\\n\\n## Documentation Updates\\nOur documentation site - [docs.phcode.dev](https://docs.phcode.dev) has received a major overhaul:\\n\\n-  Updated user guide: [docs.phcode.dev/docs/intro](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/intro)\\n-  New API documentation for extension developers: [docs.phcode.dev/api/getting-started](https://docs.phcode.dev/api/getting-started)\\n\\n## All changes\\n\\nPlease see [this link](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/commits/main/?since=2024-09-11&until=2024-11-24) for a full list of changes\\nin GitHub.\\n\\n## A Request from the Phoenix Team:\\n\\n-   **Share your feedback:** https://github.com/orgs/phcode-dev/discussions\\n-   **Spread the word** about Phoenix to friends and colleagues.\\n-   [**Consider supporting us** on GitHub Sponsors](https://github.com/sponsors/phcode-dev). Every contribution helps us\\n    to keep improving Phoenix Code.\\n\\nWith gratitude,\\n\\nThe Phoenix Team"},{"id":"release-3.9","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/release-3.9","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2024-09-02-Release-3.9/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2024-09-02-Release-3.9/index.md","title":"HTML Workflows and stability Release (September-2024, V-3.9) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.9 improves app stability and robustness, adds HTML and text editing workflow enhancements, and ChromeOS support.","date":"2024-09-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Live Preview","permalink":"/blog/tags/live-preview"},{"inline":true,"label":"Live Code Hints","permalink":"/blog/tags/live-code-hints"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":3.05,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Kiran Bose","title":"Community Contributor","url":"https://github.com/kiranbose","imageURL":"https://github.com/kiranbose.png","key":"kiran","page":null},{"name":"Devansh Agarwal","title":"Founding Engineer, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/devvaannsh","imageURL":"https://github.com/devvaannsh.png","key":"devvaannsh","page":null},{"name":"Jozsef","title":"Community Contributor","url":"https://github.com/jozsefk9","imageURL":"https://github.com/jozsefk9.png","key":"jozsefk9","page":null},{"name":"acemi1","title":"Community Contributor","url":"https://github.com/acemi1","imageURL":"https://github.com/acemi1.png","key":"acemi1","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-3.9","title":"HTML Workflows and stability Release (September-2024, V-3.9) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.9 improves app stability and robustness, adds HTML and text editing workflow enhancements, and ChromeOS support.","authors":["arun","charly","kiran","devvaannsh","jozsefk9","acemi1","phoenixBot"],"tags":["Live Preview","Live Code Hints","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Editor Experience Release (November-2024, V-3.10) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.10"},"nextItem":{"title":"Critical Update + HTML Helpers Release (June-2024, V-3.8) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.8"}},"content":"September release(3.9) of Phoenix Code is now available for download at\\n[phcode.io](https://phcode.io).\\n\\nThis month\'s core theme is `App robustness` and `stability` improvements.\\nWe have also added several `HTML and Text Editing` workflow improvements.\\n\\n## Now Available on ChromeOS\\n\\nAll new native ChromeOS app is now available on the Google Play Store.\\nThe ChromeOS app is a highly requested feature and is specially made for education and student use.\\n\\n[![google play icon (1)](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0a7f20ce-653c-43a8-ac3e-3875ea74df5b)](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=prod.phcode.twa)\\n\\n## Auto rename start and end of HTML/XML/SVG tags\\n\\nAutomatically rename paired HTML/XML/SVG tags as you type at the start or end of the tag. [Read more...](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/editing-text/#auto-rename-tag)\\n\\n![tag sync](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ad82db8c-df1c-4c83-a5db-145caab539ec)\\n\\n## Auto Tab and Spacing detection\\n\\nPhoenix Code can now automatically detect and apply the indentation style (tabs or spaces) based on the existing code in the file.\\n[Read more...](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/editing-text/#auto-space-detection)\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0adc47c5-a561-4002-bffb-d7bcde999b9d)\\n\\n## UX Improvements\\n\\n- `Reopen closed files` option added to `File` menu. Shortcut: `Ctrl-Shift-T`, or (`Cmd-Shift-T` on Mac).\\n- `Go to Next Problem` and `Go to Previous Problem` option added to [`Navigate` menu.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1732)\\n- [Tab key press to insert code hints](https://github.com/orgs/phcode-dev/discussions/1809)\\n- `window.alert`, `window.confirm` and `window.prompt` APIs [will work in live preview](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1814)\\n- Clicking on live preview no longer automatically switches from your [current unrelated file to the main HTML file.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1800)\\n- Live preview usability and navigation improvements - ([1](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1741), [2](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1760))\\n- Increase/Decrease font size will [change the Editor Font size Only](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1799).\\n- Fixed issue where code folding markers disappear when hovering over gutter in dark theme.\\n- `.cjs` and `.mjs` files support as javascript files.\\n- Support for `shtml` custom server live preview.\\n\\n## Stability Improvements\\n\\n- [Fixes node crashes after wakeup from sleep in Windows/Mac.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/issues/1791) \\n- [Kaspersky AV trojan horse false positive mitigation.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/issues/1821)\\n- [Corrupt preferences auto reset.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1818)\\n- [Boot time crash recovery.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1812)\\n- Phoenix Code won\'t start in [some Fedora/other Linux distros.](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1740)\\n\\n## Update on Release Schedule\\n\\nIn the past, we\'ve released Phoenix Code updates on a monthly basis. However, last month we encountered an issue with our release that led to stability problems for our users.\\n\\nTo address this, we\'ve taken a step back to reevaluate our release process and make improvements to ensure greater robustness and reliability going forward.\\n\\nAs a result, we\'ve decided to slow down the desktop release schedule to once every two months. This will allow us to thoroughly test each update and deliver a more stable experience.\\n\\nWe apologize for any inconvenience caused by the previous release and appreciate your patience and understanding as we work to improve the quality of Phoenix Code.\\n\\nWe look forward to bringing you new features and improvements in a more controlled and reliable manner.\\n\\n## A Request from the Phoenix Team:\\n\\n-   **Share your feedback:** https://github.com/orgs/phcode-dev/discussions\\n-   **Spread the word** about Phoenix to friends and colleagues.\\n-   [**Consider supporting us** on GitHub Sponsors](https://github.com/sponsors/phcode-dev).\\n    Every contribution helps us to keep improving Phoenix Code.\\n\\nWith gratitude,\\n\\nThe Phoenix Team"},{"id":"release-3.8","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/release-3.8","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2024-06-27-Release-3.8/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2024-06-27-Release-3.8/index.md","title":"Critical Update + HTML Helpers Release (June-2024, V-3.8) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.8 fixes crashes on M1 Macs and Linux, adds HTML drag-and-drop editing, and improves live preview stability.","date":"2024-06-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Live Preview","permalink":"/blog/tags/live-preview"},{"inline":true,"label":"Live Code Hints","permalink":"/blog/tags/live-code-hints"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":1.89,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-3.8","title":"Critical Update + HTML Helpers Release (June-2024, V-3.8) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.8 fixes crashes on M1 Macs and Linux, adds HTML drag-and-drop editing, and improves live preview stability.","authors":["arun","charly","phoenixBot"],"tags":["Live Preview","Live Code Hints","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"HTML Workflows and stability Release (September-2024, V-3.9) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.9"},"nextItem":{"title":"Little Things Release (May-2024, V-3.7) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.7"}},"content":"June release(3.8) of Phoenix Code is now available for download at\\n[phcode.io](https://phcode.io).\\n\\nThis update addresses a critical issue where Phoenix Code crashes in M1 Macs\\nat app start after a recent macOS update. Also fixes app start issues in some\\nnewer Linux distributions with latest webkitGTK.\\n\\nThis month\'s theme is all about `HTML assistance`, `Drag and Drop` and\\n`ESLint`. We\'ve also addressed numerous feature requests and bug reports, along\\nwith improving app UX and stability.\\n\\n#### HTML Validation\\n\\nWe have added HTML Validator to help you find errors like duplicate IDs,\\nunrecognized tags, and more. [Read more here.](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/Features/Problems%20Panel/html-lint/)\\n\\n![HTML validate](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/5bc4a1cc-2429-477c-83ca-91f19dcc0b0f)\\n\\n#### Drag and Drop Files and Folders in Desktop Apps - Experimental\\n\\nDrag files from Explorer (Windows) or Finder (Mac) and drop them into Phoenix\\nCode to open individual files. Drop a folder to open it as a project.\\n\\n> Note: This feature is currently disabled on Linux due to UI issues in some\\n> Linux distributions. To enable it, select menu\\n> `Debug -> Experimental Features -> Drag And Drop Files.`\\n\\n>  This feature is not yet available in the browser version @ phcode.dev \\n\\n![drag and drop](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/ddd96ff7-bc99-46a6-a62d-6f6f5b78438b)\\n\\n#### ESLint Support\\n\\nAll new and simplified ESLint experience that displays all ESLint errors in a\\nfile. Fix one or all errors with a single click. To enable, open a project that\\nuses ESLint in Phoenix Code. [Read more here.](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/Features/Problems%20Panel/ESLint/)\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/eb00691a-a0b4-4c1a-9209-d78fc7db764d)\\n\\n#### UX Improvements\\n\\n-   **Non-Intrusive Automatic Updates:** Updates are now installed in the\\n    background with no more distracting dialogs when an update is available.\\n-   **Case-Sensitive File Renaming:** Renaming files to a different case, such as\\n    `a.txt` to `A.TXT`, is now supported.\\n-   `File > New Project` is now renamed to `Start Project`.\\n-   Increased stability and app startup experience.\\n\\n## A Request from the Phoenix Team:\\n\\n-   **Share your feedback:** https://github.com/orgs/phcode-dev/discussions\\n-   **Spread the word** about Phoenix to friends and colleagues.\\n-   [**Consider supporting us** on GitHub Sponsors](https://github.com/sponsors/phcode-dev).\\n    Every contribution helps us to keep improving Phoenix Code.\\n\\nWith gratitude,\\n\\nThe Phoenix Team"},{"id":"release-3.7","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/release-3.7","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2024-05-26-Release-3.7/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2024-05-26-Release-3.7/index.md","title":"Little Things Release (May-2024, V-3.7) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.7 focuses on live preview improvements, CSS class and style code hints, and small UX refinements.","date":"2024-05-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Live Preview","permalink":"/blog/tags/live-preview"},{"inline":true,"label":"Live Code Hints","permalink":"/blog/tags/live-code-hints"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":1.54,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-3.7","title":"Little Things Release (May-2024, V-3.7) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.7 focuses on live preview improvements, CSS class and style code hints, and small UX refinements.","authors":["arun","charly","phoenixBot"],"tags":["Live Preview","Live Code Hints","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Critical Update + HTML Helpers Release (June-2024, V-3.8) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.8"},"nextItem":{"title":"April-2024 Release (V-3.6) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.6"}},"content":"May release(3.7) of Phoenix Code is now available for download at\\n[phcode.io](https://phcode.io).\\n\\nThis month\'s release is all about Live previews and the little things that make\\nyour work just a bit easier. We\'ve also addressed numerous feature requests and\\nUX bug reports, along with improving app UX and stability.\\n\\n#### Live CSS Class and Style Code Hints\\n\\nInteractively edit CSS styles and classes using the up/down arrow keys in code\\nhints. This is available in CSS files and inline CSS styles/class names in HTML\\nfiles. This provides an instant boost to your HTML and CSS workflows.\\n\\n![classLiveHints](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/502c8751-5269-4c34-9178-5ad08f8ad837)\\n\\n#### CSS/HTML Code Hints improvements\\n\\nImproved relevance of suggestions in CSS and HTML code hints. For E.g.,\\ntyping `b` in a CSS file will prioritize `background-color`. Typing `between`\\nwill bring up relevant CSS property-value combinations like `align-content: space-between;`\\n(see image below), so you don\'t have to remember which value is under which CSS property.\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/2a9f8809-de19-43cf-bdcd-d1a5def2e080)\\n\\n#### Move to trash/bin when deleting files\\n\\nDeleting files from the files panel in desktop apps will move the file/folder to\\ntrash/recycle bin instead of permanently deleting it.\\n\\n#### UX Improvements\\n\\n* Deleting the `Phoenix Code` folder in user `Documents` directory is now supported.\\n* Increased stability and app startup experience.\\n* `Copy Error Message` button is now available in the problems panel.\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/95c59e71-5ce8-4697-963e-0fa567062ab0)\\n\\n## A Request from the Phoenix Team:\\n\\n-   **Share your feedback:** https://github.com/orgs/phcode-dev/discussions\\n-   **Spread the word** about Phoenix to friends and colleagues.\\n-   [**Consider supporting us** on GitHub Sponsors](https://github.com/sponsors/phcode-dev).\\n    Every contribution helps us to keep improving Phoenix Code.\\n\\nWith gratitude,\\n\\nThe Phoenix Team"},{"id":"release-3.6","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/release-3.6","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2024-04-27-Release-3.6/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2024-04-27-Release-3.6/index.md","title":"April-2024 Release (V-3.6) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.6 adds PHP and custom live preview servers, advanced CSS/LESS/SCSS code intelligence, and UX improvements.","date":"2024-04-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Native Apps","permalink":"/blog/tags/native-apps"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":1.88,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Andrew Young","title":"Community Contributor","url":"https://github.com/ayoung5555","imageURL":"https://github.com/ayoung5555.png","key":"andrew","page":null},{"name":"Faizan Bhagat","title":"Community Contributor","url":"https://github.com/faizanbhagat","imageURL":"https://github.com/faizanbhagat.png","key":"faizan","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-3.6","title":"April-2024 Release (V-3.6) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.6 adds PHP and custom live preview servers, advanced CSS/LESS/SCSS code intelligence, and UX improvements.","authors":["arun","charly","andrew","faizan","phoenixBot"],"tags":["Native Apps","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Little Things Release (May-2024, V-3.7) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.7"},"nextItem":{"title":"March-2024 Release (V-3.5) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.5"}},"content":"April release(3.6) of Phoenix Code is now available for download at\\n[phcode.io](https://phcode.io).\\n\\nThis month\'s upgrade is big! with a lot of features you asked for - PHP/Custom\\nlive preview servers, advanced CSS/LESS/SCSS/HTML code intelligence and tooling,\\nUX and app stability improvements.\\n\\n#### Custom Live Preview Servers\\n\\nPreview PHP, React, and other dynamically rendered files with the\\nnew server settings dialog. Read more at\\n[Live Preview Settings Docs](https://docs.phcode.dev/docs/Features/live-preview-settings/).\\n\\n![Screenshot from 2024-04-12 13-08-34](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/69fa0ee4-7262-42af-97d2-26154ec4a3b9)\\n\\n#### HTML Code Intelligence\\n\\nCSS class hints are now shown within the HTML file\'s class attribute.\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/112ad909-8fd0-4fc4-8042-041ecade9481)\\n\\n#### Advanced CSS Tooling\\n\\nSupport for the latest CSS/LESS/SCSS syntax. Code intelligence and error\\ndetection for CSS, SCSS, and LESS files.\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/9c083bd3-9e34-418d-a1c8-c152393c37b2)\\n\\n#### Reverse CSS Highlighting\\n\\nClicking an element in the live preview now highlights its selector in the\\nsource CSS file editor.\\n\\n![Reverse CSS Highlighting gif](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/dcb9a111-60fa-479b-8766-dd3109b2134f)\\n\\n#### Editor Rulers\\n\\nAdd multiple, color-customizable rulers in the editor to better visualize line\\nlengths. [Read more...](/docs/customizing-editor#editor-rulers)\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/71b8b04c-d2ca-47b8-84bb-53cd0fb4593c)\\n\\n#### Open Live Preview in any Browser\\n\\nDirect buttons to open live previews in Chrome, Edge, Safari, and Firefox.\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/05f92c99-8a35-45a5-a8ee-75d581e6e96a)\\n\\n#### Docusaurus Support\\n\\nLive preview [docusaurus.io](https://docusaurus.io/) based markdown\\ndocumentation server.\\n\\n#### UX Improvements\\n\\nIncreased stability and more predictable live preview behavior.\\n\\n#### Community Contributions\\n\\n1. Changes to files and folders made externally are now automatically synced in\\n   the Browser version of Phoenix Code.\\n   ([view change](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/pull/1566)) - By\\n   [Andrew Young](https://github.com/ayoung5555),\\n   [Faizan Bhagat](https://github.com/faizanbhagat)\\n\\n## A Request from the Phoenix Team:\\n\\n-   **Share your feedback:** https://github.com/orgs/phcode-dev/discussions\\n-   **Spread the word** about Phoenix to friends and colleagues.\\n-   [**Consider supporting us** on GitHub Sponsors](https://github.com/sponsors/phcode-dev).\\n    Every contribution helps us to keep improving Phoenix Code.\\n\\nWith gratitude,\\n\\nThe Phoenix Team"},{"id":"release-3.5","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/release-3.5","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2024-03-26-Release-3.5/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2024-03-26-Release-3.5/index.md","title":"March-2024 Release (V-3.5) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.5 improves large project support, Find in Files, inline HTML number dials, and app stability. Download for Windows, Mac, and Linux.","date":"2024-03-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Native Apps","permalink":"/blog/tags/native-apps"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":1.09,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Kiran Bose","title":"Community Contributor","url":"https://github.com/kiranbose","imageURL":"https://github.com/kiranbose.png","key":"kiran","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"release-3.5","title":"March-2024 Release (V-3.5) is now Live","description":"Phoenix Code 3.5 improves large project support, Find in Files, inline HTML number dials, and app stability. Download for Windows, Mac, and Linux.","authors":["arun","charly","kiran","phoenixBot"],"tags":["Native Apps","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"April-2024 Release (V-3.6) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.6"},"nextItem":{"title":"Introducing Phoenix Code Desktop- Now on Windows, Mac & Linux","permalink":"/blog/welcome"}},"content":"The March release(3.5) of Phoenix Code is now available for download\\nat [phcode.io](https://phcode.io).\\nThis month\'s update improves support for large projects, Find in Files, and app stability.\\n\\n## Inline HTML Number Dials\\n\\nYou can now use number dials in inline HTML styles, not just in CSS files.\\n\\n![image](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/f56ab73b-c080-4f01-a083-f41aa10c3d16)\\n\\n## Search Filters - Advanced Find in Files\\n\\nAll new search filters to find exactly what you want. `Search in files` or\\n`Exclude files` matching the given pattern instantly.\\n\\n![new find in files](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/assets/5336369/9a46a6a8-01a2-45db-aebc-9b280977bdc1)\\n\\n## Other Improvements\\n\\n-   Projects with very large number of files can now be opened.\\n-   Performance improvement when opening minified CSS/JS files and other formats\\n    with very long lines.\\n-   Added support for beautifying SCSS, SASS, LESS, TSX, and JSX files. The\\n    beautification feature now preserves the cursor position.\\n-   Improved startup speed and overall app stability.\\n-   Bug fixes and improvements to new project dialog.\\n\\n\\n## A Request from the Phoenix Team:\\n * **Share your feedback:** https://github.com/orgs/phcode-dev/discussions\\n * **Spread the word** about Phoenix to friends and colleagues.\\n * [**Consider supporting us** on Open Collective](https://opencollective.com/phoenix-ide). Every contribution helps us to keep improving and expanding Phoenix Code.\\n\\nWith gratitude,\\n\\nThe Phoenix Team"},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/phcode-dev/docs/blob/main/blog/2024-03-05-welcome/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2024-03-05-welcome/index.md","title":"Introducing Phoenix Code Desktop- Now on Windows, Mac & Linux","description":"Phoenix Code native desktop apps are now available for Windows, Mac, and Linux. Web version available for ChromeOS and other platforms.","date":"2024-03-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"hello world","permalink":"/blog/tags/hello-world"},{"inline":true,"label":"Native Apps","permalink":"/blog/tags/native-apps"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/blog/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mac","permalink":"/blog/tags/mac"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release"}],"readingTime":2.64,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Arun Bose","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/abose","imageURL":"https://github.com/abose.png","key":"arun","page":null},{"name":"Charly P Abraham","title":"Co-Founder, Phoenix Code","url":"https://github.com/charlypa","imageURL":"https://github.com/charlypa.png","key":"charly","page":null},{"name":"Kiran Bose","title":"Community Contributor","url":"https://github.com/kiranbose","imageURL":"https://github.com/kiranbose.png","key":"kiran","page":null},{"name":"Mathew Dennis","title":"Quality Control & Social Media","url":"https://github.com/mathewdennis1","imageURL":"https://github.com/mathewdennis1.png","key":"mathew","page":null},{"name":"Phoenix Code Bot","title":"Our little elf who keeps the whole place together","url":"https://github.com/phoenixide","imageURL":"https://github.com/phoenixide.png","key":"phoenixBot","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Introducing Phoenix Code Desktop- Now on Windows, Mac & Linux","description":"Phoenix Code native desktop apps are now available for Windows, Mac, and Linux. Web version available for ChromeOS and other platforms.","authors":["arun","charly","kiran","mathew","phoenixBot"],"tags":["hello world","Native Apps","Windows","Linux","Mac","Release"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"March-2024 Release (V-3.5) is now Live","permalink":"/blog/release-3.5"}},"content":"We\'re thrilled to share a huge update with you all today - Phoenix Code native apps are officially here! \ud83c\udf89 . Get your copy from [phcode.io](https://phcode.io) !\\n\\nWe have been working hard over the last several months to make native apps possible. It took a lot of effort and perseverance with our limited resources to reach this far. We hope to have your support in doing more :)\\n\\nWith this update,  Phoenix Code can now run almost anywhere! Native apps are available for Windows, Mac and most Linux distributions. For Chrome-OS and other platforms, simply use the web browser version; no install needed.\\n\\n## Looking Back\\n\\nThe team building Phoenix Code started its history as some of the initial team members building Brackets at Adobe from 2014. Phoenix Code took over the full time development of Brackets in 2021. We had a mammoth task to bring Brackets up to date with the latest web technologies. [The result was Phoenix Code](https://www.reddit.com/r/brackets/comments/tz06tn/brackets_community_announcement_phoenix_code/)- A Platform rewrite of Brackets that allowed it to run on any Web Browser. But our users were pretty clear that a desktop app was needed.\\n\\n It was at this time that this hot new rust based tech called [`Tauri framework`](https://tauri.app/) came into the picture and we finally had the missing piece to build a truly modern and light-weight desktop app. Fast forward to 2024 and Phoenix Code now runs *almost* anywhere(*almost* as we are one step short with touch and mobile screens).\\n\\nPhoenix Code marks the first large-scale, truly independent release from the Brackets community. Entirely homegrown within the community, it is also the largest engineering effort put into Brackets since 2015 (including Adobe).\\n\\n## Acknowledgements\\n\\nThis release is made possible by the Phoenix Code Team- [Arun](https://github.com/abose), [Charly](https://github.com/charlypa) and [Mathew](https://github.com/mathewdennis1). Our shiny new website [phcode.io](https://phcode.io) is built by community contribution from [Kiran](https://github.com/kiranbose).\\n\\nWe would also like to thank the S-tier community support provided by the [Tauri](https://tauri.app/) development team, esp. [Fabian](https://github.com/FabianLars).\\n\\n## Looking Ahead\\n\\n> Our guiding principle is to make coding as intuitive and fun as playing a video game - for web developers, designers, and students.\\n\\nWith the native apps now available, we\'re starting the next phase of our journey. Beginning this month, there will be\\na new update of Phoenix Code released every month with all the features that you [requested](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/issues?q=is%3Aissue+is%3Aopen+label%3A%22customer+raised%22) and [more](https://github.com/phcode-dev/phoenix/issues?q=is%3Aissue+is%3Aopen+label%3A%22Feature+Request%22).\\n\\nHowever, our ambitions come with costs, and it\'s your support that makes all the difference.\\n\\n## How You Can Support:\\n * **Share your feedback:** https://github.com/orgs/phcode-dev/discussions\\n * **Spread the word** about Phoenix to friends and colleagues.\\n * [**Consider supporting us** on Open Collective](https://opencollective.com/phoenix-ide). Every contribution helps us to keep improving and expanding Phoenix Code.\\n\\nWe\'re grateful for the incredible support this community has shown us so far. Let\'s continue to make Phoenix Code even better, together.\\n\\nWith gratitude,\\n\\nThe Phoenix Team"}]}}')}}]);